WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.944 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.944 --> 00:00:03.402 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:04.860 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:17.030 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.750 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Today, I am
picking back up with a gap

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.800 align:middle line:90%
in between on advertising.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.410 align:middle line:84%
And so the plan is
today, I'm going

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:28.040 align:middle line:84%
to talk about empirical
work on advertising.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.690 align:middle line:84%
And then talking about
empirical advertising,

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.230 align:middle line:84%
it's a big challenge
for a class like this

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.363 align:middle line:84%
because marketing is
a field unto itself.

00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:39.530 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know if there
are half as many marketing

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.720 align:middle line:84%
economists as there
are economists

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:46.850 align:middle line:84%
of all-- you know, economists
of all fields together.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:51.320 align:middle line:84%
You know, much of marketing
is an overwhelmingly empirical

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:51.900 align:middle line:90%
field.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.670 align:middle line:84%
If you look at what
marketing faculty do,

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.680 align:middle line:84%
you know, there's
multiple motivations.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.850 align:middle line:84%
One is there is tremendous
demand from practitioners

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.990 align:middle line:84%
to understand the
value of advertising.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.170 align:middle line:84%
You know, this is
what advertisers

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.140 align:middle line:84%
want to do is know how valuable
the advertising they're buying

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:09.990 align:middle line:90%
is.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.540 align:middle line:84%
There are many
consulting firms who

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.730 align:middle line:84%
try to help them figure out
how valuable the marketing is,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:19.380 align:middle line:84%
and there are many academics who
try to help the consultants try

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.630 align:middle line:84%
to help develop new
cutting-edge techniques for how

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.420 align:middle line:84%
you would measure the
effectiveness of advertising.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.910 align:middle line:84%
But then there's also a
substantial marketing literature

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:30.000 align:middle line:84%
that is about basic
economic questions.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000 align:middle line:84%
You know, as I said, we have
these different ways in which

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.970 align:middle line:84%
advertising could be effective,
whether it's changing tastes

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.790 align:middle line:84%
or whether it's
providing information

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.930 align:middle line:84%
or whether it's
signaling something.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.882 align:middle line:84%
And you know, I think we
want to know why the--

00:01:44.882 --> 00:01:47.340 align:middle line:84%
sometimes those effects, the
welfare effects were different

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.180 align:middle line:84%
depending on what
advertising was doing,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.210 align:middle line:84%
so I think there's also
academic interest in,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.060 align:middle line:84%
what is marketing-- what
is advertising doing?

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.760 align:middle line:84%
And what are its general
equilibrium effects

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.180 align:middle line:90%
on the markets involved?

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.212 align:middle line:84%
So you see many different
methodologies going on.

00:02:05.212 --> 00:02:09.870 align:middle line:84%
Marketing faculty at places,
top places like Sloan,

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.690 align:middle line:84%
a number of them have
relationships with advertisers

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.310 align:middle line:84%
or with advertising
firms that let

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.100 align:middle line:84%
them do sophisticated
experimental work

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.780 align:middle line:84%
to evaluate advertising
with that advantage.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.920 align:middle line:84%
There's also-- you
know, if you look at IO,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.260 align:middle line:84%
it's not like IO is
really ahead of marketing

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.220 align:middle line:84%
in structural demand models
for understanding consumer

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.340 align:middle line:84%
preferences and how A,
affects consumer utility.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.770 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot of
complicated structural stuff.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:42.780 align:middle line:84%
What's actually sort of funny
is the more recent trend

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.070 align:middle line:84%
in marketing is there's actually
been this recent rise in just

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.650 align:middle line:84%
plain vanilla causal
inference techniques

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.260 align:middle line:84%
and using plain vanilla
causal inference techniques

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.100 align:middle line:90%
to try to evaluate advertising.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:58.560 align:middle line:84%
And in some ways, when
you're trying to talk about,

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.470 align:middle line:84%
what's the general equilibrium
effects of advertising,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.020 align:middle line:84%
like, if you allow
advertising in a market,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.380 align:middle line:84%
how does it affect
market prices, you know,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.870 align:middle line:84%
you can't do that
experimentally because it's

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.170 align:middle line:90%
a market-wide question.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.050 align:middle line:84%
And so for those
market wide questions,

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:17.520 align:middle line:84%
we're seeing more people do just
simple difference-in-difference

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.670 align:middle line:84%
and other causal techniques
to try to figure out,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.866 align:middle line:84%
what are market-level
effects of advertising?

00:03:22.866 --> 00:03:26.998 align:middle line:90%


00:03:26.998 --> 00:03:27.540 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.270 align:middle line:84%
Catherine Tucker in Sloan, she
has a recent survey article

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.730 align:middle line:84%
discussing a number of recent
papers that have done this.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.850 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
start with, first,

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:39.180 align:middle line:84%
one of the earlier of these
difference-in-difference papers.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.200 align:middle line:90%
This is Milyo and Waldfogel.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.550 align:middle line:84%
So you know, the paper title
is "Effect of Price Advertising

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.670 align:middle line:84%
on Prices, Evidence in the
Wake of 44 Liquormart."

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.370 align:middle line:84%
44 Liquormart, I think, is the
name of a Rhode Island liquor

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:54.480 align:middle line:90%
store.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.570 align:middle line:84%
And the backstory of this
is from 1956 to 1996,

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:03.550 align:middle line:84%
Rhode Island banned all
advertising of alcohol prices.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.460 align:middle line:84%
So these kinds of
circulars you'd

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.780 align:middle line:84%
get in the mail of liquor
warehouse mix and match.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.810 align:middle line:84%
Buy this many bottles, you know,
Sam Adams, $7.99 a six-pack,

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.635 align:middle line:84%
this was all illegal
in Rhode Island.

00:04:16.635 --> 00:04:18.740 align:middle line:84%
This was also illegal
in Rhode Island.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.490 align:middle line:84%
You could not in the
state of Rhode Island

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:24.000 align:middle line:84%
have a sign in your
liquor store window

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.760 align:middle line:84%
visible from the street showing
how expensive the beer was.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.010 align:middle line:84%
So it was a just
complete, total ban

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:33.250 align:middle line:84%
on any advertising
of alcohol prices.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:37.570 align:middle line:84%
And a liquor store, I
think called 44 Liquormart,

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.270 align:middle line:84%
sued the state of
Rhode Island, saying

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:44.080 align:middle line:84%
that this was a
ban-- an infringement

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.470 align:middle line:90%
on their freedom of speech.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.535 align:middle line:84%
And in 1996, the Supreme
Court overturned the law

00:04:48.535 --> 00:04:50.660 align:middle line:84%
as an unwarranted restriction
on freedom of speech.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.550 align:middle line:84%
And so starting on that
one date, May 13, 1996,

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:58.420 align:middle line:84%
advertising alcohol was legal
in the state of Rhode Island.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:04:59.920 align:middle line:84%
And so what Milyo
and Waldfogel were

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.350 align:middle line:84%
trying to do is do a
difference-in-difference

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.690 align:middle line:84%
approach to see,
how does that affect

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.690 align:middle line:90%
alcohol pricing in the state?

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:09.280 align:middle line:90%
And I can say there's actually--

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.800 align:middle line:84%
in some sense,
they're building--

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.780 align:middle line:90%
there's an earlier paper--

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.340 align:middle line:84%
there's a classic paper from the
1970s by Benham on advertising

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.160 align:middle line:90%
eyeglasses.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.060 align:middle line:84%
So some states allow you to
advertise eyeglass prices,

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:23.750 align:middle line:90%
and some states do not.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:25.960 align:middle line:84%
And Benham has this
classic paper from 1970s,

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:30.640 align:middle line:84%
basically just a cross-sectional
regression showing that glasses

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.070 align:middle line:84%
cost 20% less in states where
you're allowed to advertise

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:35.630 align:middle line:84%
eyeglass prices than
ones that you don't.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.840 align:middle line:84%
And so I think that was--
part of the background is,

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.298 align:middle line:84%
is there really a big-- going
to be a big effect on alcohol

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:42.370 align:middle line:90%
prices of allowing advertising?

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.905 align:middle line:84%
Which, if your
view of advertising

00:05:44.905 --> 00:05:47.137 align:middle line:84%
is this is reducing
consumer search costs

00:05:47.137 --> 00:05:49.220 align:middle line:84%
and consumer search costs
are big part of markups,

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:52.060 align:middle line:84%
you could think advertising
would have a big effect there.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.680 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I think the ambitious
thing that Milyo and Waldfogel

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.680 align:middle line:84%
did on this paper
was they gambled

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.180 align:middle line:84%
on the result of the
Supreme Court case.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:03.880 align:middle line:84%
And basically, they
just-- you know,

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.800 align:middle line:84%
it was Rhode Island
in the 1990s,

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.460 align:middle line:84%
it wasn't like every liquor
store was big and had

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.830 align:middle line:84%
supermarket scanners and you
could make contracts with

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.790 align:middle line:84%
a scanner company to
get all the prices.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:17.260 align:middle line:84%
So what they started doing
is just manually collecting

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.860 align:middle line:84%
prices for 33 different
alcoholic beverages at 115

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.440 align:middle line:90%
liquor stores in June 1995.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:26.230 align:middle line:84%
So this was basically--
the Supreme Court

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:29.890 align:middle line:84%
announced that it would
take the case in June.

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.110 align:middle line:84%
They had no idea when
the case would get taken,

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:33.280 align:middle line:90%
what the decision would be.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.560 align:middle line:84%
If the law was
upheld, they would

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.267 align:middle line:90%
have had no paper whatsoever.

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:39.100 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, they just
started gathering data

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:43.725 align:middle line:84%
on liquor store prices
in anticipation of--

00:06:43.725 --> 00:06:46.378 align:middle line:84%
in anticipation of the
law being overturned.

00:06:46.378 --> 00:06:48.170 align:middle line:84%
And to do a
difference-in-difference thing,

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.450 align:middle line:84%
they did a control group
of Massachusetts stores.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.290 align:middle line:84%
They picked Rhode Island stores
and then Massachusetts stores,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.240 align:middle line:84%
some of which are near the Rhode
Island border and some of which

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.180 align:middle line:90%
are not.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.360 align:middle line:84%
And then they can do a
difference-in-difference

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:04.030 align:middle line:84%
and compare, how do prices in
Massachusetts and Rhode Island

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:05.500 align:middle line:90%
change when the liquor store--

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.200 align:middle line:84%
when the advertising
ban's overturned?

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:10.156 align:middle line:90%
In addition to these--

00:07:10.156 --> 00:07:15.850 align:middle line:84%
and let me say, the price data
collection is pretty impressive.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:19.700 align:middle line:84%
It's like they just went to
the liquor stores personally.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:22.510 align:middle line:84%
And this is something
I think also mentioned

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:23.980 align:middle line:90%
in Alan Sorensen's paper.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.282 align:middle line:84%
You can't take a clipboard
into a liquor store

00:07:26.282 --> 00:07:28.240 align:middle line:84%
and start writing down
the prices of everything

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.357 align:middle line:84%
that they sell and walk
around for 15 minutes,

00:07:30.357 --> 00:07:31.940 align:middle line:84%
writing down all the
prices they sell.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.670 align:middle line:90%
They kick you out of the store.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:36.520 align:middle line:84%
So what they actually did
is they would go in there,

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.255 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes they would
have a set of prices.

00:07:39.255 --> 00:07:41.380 align:middle line:84%
They'd then try to memorize
them, and go-- walk in,

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.005 align:middle line:90%
memorize them, and walk out.

00:07:43.005 --> 00:07:44.380 align:middle line:84%
Joel also has
these stories of he

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.180 align:middle line:90%
would bring his child with him.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.870 align:middle line:84%
And you can spend
longer if you've

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:51.370 align:middle line:84%
got a four-year-old wandering
around the aisles than you can

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.130 align:middle line:84%
as a person and put a voice
recorder in his pocket

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:56.710 align:middle line:84%
and just read out
to himself, like,

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:01.930 align:middle line:84%
the Bacardi's $8.99 or whatever
until he collected the prices.

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:03.913 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, they did this
for a year beforehand.

00:08:03.913 --> 00:08:05.330 align:middle line:84%
They did it for a
year afterwards.

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:06.830 align:middle line:90%
That's the main data set.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:08.752 align:middle line:84%
But then they also have
several other things

00:08:08.752 --> 00:08:09.710 align:middle line:90%
they were able to find.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:11.793 align:middle line:84%
They have Massachusetts
and Rhode Island both have

00:08:11.793 --> 00:08:13.610 align:middle line:90%
regulated wholesale prices.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:16.393 align:middle line:84%
They got the wholesale
prices in the stores.

00:08:16.393 --> 00:08:18.310 align:middle line:84%
They gathered data on
whether the Rhode Island

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:20.680 align:middle line:84%
stores used print
advertising, whether they

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:22.640 align:middle line:90%
used window advertisements.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.220 align:middle line:84%
And they also got
lottery ticket sales.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.740 align:middle line:84%
And the idea was that
they didn't have any way

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.290 align:middle line:84%
to gather quantity that the
liquor stores were selling,

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.240 align:middle line:84%
but they could get
data from the state

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.419 align:middle line:84%
on how many lottery tickets
were sold at each liquor store.

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:37.919 align:middle line:84%
And they're going
to use the lottery

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:39.330 align:middle line:90%
tickets as another prox--

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.070 align:middle line:84%
a rough proxy for
quantity, figuring

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.848 align:middle line:84%
that if more people come into
the store to buy alcohol,

00:08:43.848 --> 00:08:45.390 align:middle line:84%
more people are
going to buy liquor--

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.228 align:middle line:84%
buy lottery tickets
at the stores.

00:08:47.228 --> 00:08:49.020 align:middle line:84%
And that they could
get for all stores that

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.895 align:middle line:84%
sold lottery tickets,
which is a lot of them.

00:08:50.895 --> 00:08:53.310 align:middle line:90%


00:08:53.310 --> 00:09:00.340 align:middle line:84%
So here's a rough schematic
of what they were doing.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:06.330 align:middle line:84%
So their early data
collections, they're

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.147 align:middle line:90%
visiting 20 stores in June.

00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:10.230 align:middle line:84%
Then they do a bigger data
collection in September

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:12.152 align:middle line:90%
and visit 30 or 40 stores.

00:09:12.152 --> 00:09:14.610 align:middle line:84%
They get tired waiting around
for the law to be overturned.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.610 align:middle line:84%
They only visit 10
or 15 in February.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.740 align:middle line:84%
And then once the
law is overturned,

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:21.330 align:middle line:84%
then they ramp up
the data collection.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:27.430 align:middle line:84%
They're collecting from 40
or 50 stores in each wave.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:29.180 align:middle line:90%
Advertising.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.180 align:middle line:84%
You can see that
advertising didn't actually

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:32.750 align:middle line:90%
get all that big.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:40.090 align:middle line:84%
So there are-- you know,
in their sample stores,

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:42.680 align:middle line:90%
it's like three, one, five, six.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.200 align:middle line:84%
So it's less than
10% of the stores

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.965 align:middle line:84%
are choosing to advertise
once advertising is allowed.

00:09:46.965 --> 00:09:49.810 align:middle line:90%


00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:50.590 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:53.110 align:middle line:84%
So then they collected by
hand whether the stores were

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.330 align:middle line:90%
advertising, but it was not--

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:57.640 align:middle line:84%
you know, many stores
which obviously had not

00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:59.170 align:middle line:84%
been advertising
for 40 years just

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.710 align:middle line:84%
went along happily not
advertising once the law ended.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:04.620 align:middle line:90%


00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.350 align:middle line:90%
Here's an example of--

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:11.370 align:middle line:84%
these are the products
that were in their study.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:16.990 align:middle line:84%
So they have a bunch of
different standard liquor items.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:18.610 align:middle line:84%
They have several
different beers.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:22.380 align:middle line:84%
They have a bunch of
relatively-- a bunch

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:23.950 align:middle line:90%
of relatively cheap wine.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:28.711 align:middle line:84%
They have a few champagnes
and sparkling wines.

00:10:28.711 --> 00:10:31.200 align:middle line:84%
And again, the goal
of the data collection

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.450 align:middle line:84%
was to walk around the store
and get all these prices.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:34.610 align:middle line:84%
If they couldn't get
all these prices,

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:36.510 align:middle line:84%
they just got some
subset of these prices.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.530 align:middle line:84%
And that's why you can see
the number of observations

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.215 align:middle line:90%
differ a lot across product.

00:10:41.215 --> 00:10:46.310 align:middle line:90%


00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:46.810 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:51.320 align:middle line:84%
So what does-- what
does advertising do?

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:54.820 align:middle line:84%
So result 1, you
know, they're going

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.590 align:middle line:84%
to estimate the
effect of alcohol--

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:57.942 align:middle line:90%
the end of the alcohol ban.

00:10:57.942 --> 00:10:59.650 align:middle line:84%
Simple difference-in-difference
estimate.

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.900 align:middle line:84%
You just do prices
in Rhode Island

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.910 align:middle line:84%
after minus price
in Rhode Island

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.080 align:middle line:84%
before minus price
in Massachusetts

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:07.870 align:middle line:84%
after price in
Massachusetts before.

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:11.200 align:middle line:84%
It's a regression with time
fixed effects and state fixed

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.700 align:middle line:84%
effects and then a Rhode
Island times post dummy,

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:15.370 align:middle line:84%
and that's the thing
you're estimating.

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:18.940 align:middle line:84%
And what they find
is that, you know,

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:24.200 align:middle line:84%
the price was very low-- the
difference was very, very low.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.385 align:middle line:84%
So you put in the state product
and the store fixed effect,

00:11:27.385 --> 00:11:29.860 align:middle line:84%
and the prices on
average in their sample

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:33.220 align:middle line:84%
are going down by, like,
half a percent to 1%

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:36.430 align:middle line:84%
after the advertising
ban comes into effect.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:42.100 align:middle line:84%
So it's just advertising
leads to very,

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:44.120 align:middle line:84%
very slightly-- allowing
advertising leads

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:45.620 align:middle line:84%
to very, very
slightly lower prices.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.320 align:middle line:84%
And this is-- you know, they're
tracking it for one year

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:48.368 align:middle line:90%
afterwards.

00:11:48.368 --> 00:11:50.410 align:middle line:84%
So you get one year
afterwards, and within a year

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.895 align:middle line:84%
this effectively had very
little impact on retail prices.

00:11:53.895 --> 00:11:59.590 align:middle line:90%


00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:01.580 align:middle line:84%
Breaking it down
product by product,

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:04.285 align:middle line:90%
how do advertisements work?

00:12:04.285 --> 00:12:06.850 align:middle line:84%
When stores choose
to advertise, they

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.740 align:middle line:90%
advertise much lower prices.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:13.120 align:middle line:84%
So the advertised items
at an advertising store

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.500 align:middle line:84%
are roughly 20% lower than
the prices of those items

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:20.470 align:middle line:84%
at the Massachusetts stores or
the non-advertising Rhode Island

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:21.350 align:middle line:90%
stores.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:26.860 align:middle line:84%
So stores that advertise
do set very low prices.

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.720 align:middle line:84%
And then your question
might be, what's

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:32.200 align:middle line:84%
the aggregate effect
of the advertising?

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.720 align:middle line:84%
You know, as I said, I did
a loss leader model saying,

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:36.400 align:middle line:84%
like, you know, you're going
to advertise very low prices

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:37.780 align:middle line:84%
for turkeys at
Thanksgiving, and that

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:40.450 align:middle line:84%
doesn't mean that Thanksgiving
isn't the single most expensive

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:43.125 align:middle line:84%
day of the year to shop because
you can just use that as a loss

00:12:43.125 --> 00:12:44.500 align:middle line:84%
leader, bring
people in, and then

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:47.185 align:middle line:84%
advertise-- raise the
price of everything else.

00:12:47.185 --> 00:12:49.010 align:middle line:90%
That's not what's happening.

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:52.750 align:middle line:84%
So the non-advertised
prices at advertising stores

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:58.900 align:middle line:84%
are within a fraction of a
percent of the prices elsewhere.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.920 align:middle line:84%
And if anything,
they're slightly lower.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:03.250 align:middle line:84%
So it's not like the stores
advertise and jack up

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:04.500 align:middle line:90%
the prices of everything else.

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.030 align:middle line:84%
What they do is they
advertise low prices

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:10.360 align:middle line:84%
and basically leave the prices
of everything else unchanged,

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.180 align:middle line:84%
or if anything,
slightly decrease them.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:15.440 align:middle line:90%


00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:20.980 align:middle line:84%
And the non-advertising stores
in Rhode Island, their prices

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.300 align:middle line:84%
are now going down
by, like, 0.2%.

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:26.590 align:middle line:84%
So whatever the
competitive effect is,

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:29.870 align:middle line:84%
the non-advertising stores
are just not reacting--

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.950 align:middle line:84%
they're not reacting to the
advertising of their rivals

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:37.180 align:middle line:84%
other than this-- you know,
just very, very tiny cut

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:39.710 align:middle line:90%
in average prices.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:41.010 align:middle line:90%
Other things in the paper.

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.680 align:middle line:90%
So it wasn't in the table.

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:45.060 align:middle line:84%
Rivals don't respond
to advertised prices.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:51.410 align:middle line:84%
So you advertise, I've got
Bud Light, 20% off this week.

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:54.250 align:middle line:84%
Your rival stores don't
have Bud Light at 20% off.

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:58.490 align:middle line:84%
They basically just don't react
at all to the advertising.

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:06.340 align:middle line:84%
On the quantity effects,
advertising stores' share

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:10.840 align:middle line:84%
of lottery ticket sales
went from 16.4% to 18.4%

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.520 align:middle line:90%
after the ban ends.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:16.300 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember, it looked
like the advertising levels

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:17.480 align:middle line:90%
were about 10%.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:21.970 align:middle line:84%
So the advertising stores
were bigger stores.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:25.270 align:middle line:84%
They were selling more than
10% of the lottery tickets,

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:27.220 align:middle line:84%
but they go from
16% to 18%, so that

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:31.180 align:middle line:84%
suggests that their
quantities are going up.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:33.610 align:middle line:90%
But again, if you think about--

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:37.420 align:middle line:84%
so the fraction-- the half
a percent or the 0.7%,

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:41.620 align:middle line:84%
that was the price on a
just item-weighted basis.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.660 align:middle line:84%
You could-- your question
would be, well, OK,

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:46.480 align:middle line:84%
on a sale-weighted
basis, could the drops

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:49.720 align:middle line:84%
in prices that consumers
pay be much larger?

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:53.620 align:middle line:84%
You know, this is
suggesting that it's only 2%

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:55.100 align:middle line:84%
of the demand that
may be moving.

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:57.610 align:middle line:84%
And so if only 2% of the
demand may be moving,

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:00.580 align:middle line:84%
the aggregate effect on
consumers is probably small.

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.520 align:middle line:84%
Now, if that 2% were all
getting stuff that was 20% off,

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:06.560 align:middle line:84%
you know, still, 2%
of the people buying

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:08.960 align:middle line:84%
stuff that's 20% off,
that's, like-- what is that?

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:10.080 align:middle line:90%
0.4%?

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.280 align:middle line:90%
So it's not a very big effect.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:16.730 align:middle line:84%
A second thing that they note is
that the stores that eventually

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:23.450 align:middle line:84%
advertise were charging 7%
less before the advertising

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:24.750 align:middle line:90%
ban was eliminated.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:27.380 align:middle line:84%
So these larger stores were
the larger, cheaper stores

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.090 align:middle line:90%
to begin with.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:32.990 align:middle line:84%
So again, if 2% of the
people are moving and buying

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:36.255 align:middle line:84%
stuff that's on average
7% less, that's,

00:15:36.255 --> 00:15:37.880 align:middle line:84%
again, adding a few
tenths of a percent

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:39.890 align:middle line:90%
to what consumers are saving.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:45.585 align:middle line:84%
So I think in aggregate, it
looks like this shift-- consumer

00:15:45.585 --> 00:15:47.210 align:middle line:84%
shift to lower priced
stores presumably

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:49.710 align:middle line:84%
within the advertising stores,
consumers buy the things that

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:51.930 align:middle line:90%
are the 20% off price levels.

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:57.410 align:middle line:84%
But on aggregate, average
prices in an unweighted sense

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:58.140 align:middle line:90%
don't change.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:01.220 align:middle line:84%
And on a-- whatever, consumers
are better off sense,

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:03.745 align:middle line:84%
consumers are making a
couple percent off this.

00:16:03.745 --> 00:16:06.620 align:middle line:90%


00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:07.370 align:middle line:90%
So I don't know.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:10.580 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if this makes us
think that all of these search

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.940 align:middle line:84%
costs for consumers, at least
for buying beer and wine

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:17.330 align:middle line:84%
and liquor, are not such a big
deal or such a big determinant

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:20.270 align:middle line:84%
of markups, or at
least the advertising

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:23.405 align:middle line:84%
is limited effectiveness in
changing those consumer search

00:16:23.405 --> 00:16:23.905 align:middle line:90%
costs.

00:16:23.905 --> 00:16:28.840 align:middle line:90%


00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:30.700 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:31.210 align:middle line:90%
Nope?

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:33.500 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:36.230 align:middle line:84%
So I thought next
thing I would do

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:38.690 align:middle line:84%
is go to the other
extreme in data

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:40.610 align:middle line:84%
and just give you a
sense of, you know,

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.650 align:middle line:84%
what are the kinds of
papers that people are

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:44.645 align:middle line:90%
writing with good access to--

00:16:44.645 --> 00:16:47.360 align:middle line:90%


00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:53.510 align:middle line:84%
good access to firms that allow
them to do experimental work?

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:56.387 align:middle line:84%
So this is a paper by Randall
Lewis and David Reiley,

00:16:56.387 --> 00:16:58.970 align:middle line:84%
"Online Ads and Offline Sales,
Measuring the Effects of Retail

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:01.910 align:middle line:84%
Advertising via
Controlled Experiment."

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:05.119 align:middle line:84%
So at the time that they
started working on this paper,

00:17:05.119 --> 00:17:08.450 align:middle line:84%
Randall and David were both
employees of Yahoo or working

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:10.661 align:middle line:90%
at Yahoo research.

00:17:10.661 --> 00:17:11.869 align:middle line:90%
I think-- well, I don't know.

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:16.121 align:middle line:84%
This is-- I guess it's
funny to be lecturing

00:17:16.121 --> 00:17:18.329 align:middle line:84%
a class of people so young
they probably don't really

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:19.329 align:middle line:90%
remember Yahoo.

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:25.770 align:middle line:84%
But in the year 2000, Yahoo
had 25% of all page views

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:26.838 align:middle line:90%
on the internet.

00:17:26.838 --> 00:17:28.380 align:middle line:84%
So if you think
about, what do people

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:29.713 align:middle line:90%
do when they're on the internet?

00:17:29.713 --> 00:17:32.170 align:middle line:84%
And the year 2000 was
people were on Yahoo.

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:35.910 align:middle line:84%
And it was like, you know,
Yahoo was just like--

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:41.830 align:middle line:84%
Yahoo was what Facebook and
Google and Amazon-- whatever.

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:44.790 align:middle line:84%
Like, at the time in 2000, Yahoo
was the dominant online firm

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.312 align:middle line:90%
and people are like--

00:17:46.312 --> 00:17:48.520 align:middle line:84%
Yahoo was their home page,
and they go through Yahoo,

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:51.810 align:middle line:84%
and they would look through
Yahoo shopping Yahoo

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:54.810 align:middle line:90%
mail, Yahoo whatever.

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:59.730 align:middle line:84%
This is what the Yahoo
front page looked like.

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:03.240 align:middle line:84%
Yahoo front page, it was
kind of like a news site,

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:04.330 align:middle line:90%
your starting page.

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:06.930 align:middle line:84%
You can see over here,
you had other things

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:11.610 align:middle line:84%
you could do on Yahoo like Yahoo
Autos, Answers, Jobs, Groups,

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:15.400 align:middle line:84%
Mobile Web, Movies,
TV, whatever.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:18.920 align:middle line:84%
But it was mostly
like a news page.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:22.660 align:middle line:84%
And then they would have these
display ads like this one.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:24.940 align:middle line:84%
This is what they
call an LREC ad, which

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.490 align:middle line:90%
stands for Large Rectangle.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.190 align:middle line:84%
You'd have this large rectangle
on the front page with some ad.

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.890 align:middle line:84%
You then have other smaller
ads here on the front page.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:37.180 align:middle line:84%
And as you went through, if you
read the news pages or whatever,

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:38.980 align:middle line:84%
there would be
more ads like this

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:40.880 align:middle line:90%
appearing on subsequent pages.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:44.370 align:middle line:84%
And Yahoo made its
money by selling these.

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:47.080 align:middle line:90%


00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:49.690 align:middle line:90%
How did those get sold?

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:54.110 align:middle line:84%
Yahoo sold those ads in
multiple different ways.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:59.600 align:middle line:84%
The front page ad there was
actually sold by the day.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:02.200 align:middle line:84%
So every day, there
would be a new advertiser

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:03.710 align:middle line:90%
on the Yahoo front page.

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.020 align:middle line:84%
Some days it would
be a 50/50 split.

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:09.490 align:middle line:84%
50% of page views would
be ad A. 50% of the pages

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:11.998 align:middle line:84%
would be ad B. Other days,
it would be everybody.

00:19:11.998 --> 00:19:14.290 align:middle line:84%
But basically, you could just
run a one-day ad campaign

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:18.070 align:middle line:84%
on Yahoo and they'd sell you
that LREC on the front page.

00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:21.470 align:middle line:84%
When you got to the
inside pages on Yahoo,

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:25.010 align:middle line:84%
there were many, many, many
different ways to bid for ads.

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:29.170 align:middle line:84%
Like, you could buy
an ad that would--

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:31.570 align:middle line:84%
if I'm Netflix or
whatever, I could

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:35.080 align:middle line:84%
buy an ad that appears on pages
that mention a movie title.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:37.220 align:middle line:84%
And like, I want
to buy movie title.

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.950 align:middle line:84%
Or you could buy ads
targeted to consumers.

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:42.920 align:middle line:90%
I want women 18 to 39.

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:47.440 align:middle line:84%
I want men 25 to 54, and I'm
willing to bid this much if it's

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:48.760 align:middle line:90%
someone in that category.

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:51.990 align:middle line:84%
You could bid
re-targeting kinds of ads.

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:53.740 align:middle line:84%
Like, I don't know if
you've ever noticed.

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:56.590 align:middle line:84%
You look at a pair of shoes
on Zappos or something,

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.530 align:middle line:84%
and then those shoes follow
you around the internet.

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:02.350 align:middle line:84%
You can bid, I'm
paying $5 per 1,000.

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:04.740 align:middle line:84%
Anytime someone who's
visited my site and puts

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:06.490 align:middle line:84%
something in their
cart and not bought it,

00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:09.050 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to buy an ad
and target it there.

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:11.608 align:middle line:84%
So you can target by the
individual characteristics.

00:20:11.608 --> 00:20:13.400 align:middle line:84%
You can target by the
content of the pages.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:16.110 align:middle line:84%
You can target on the person's
past behavior using cookies,

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:21.270 align:middle line:84%
but they would sell
all those things.

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:22.740 align:middle line:84%
This particular
experiment they do,

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:28.238 align:middle line:84%
they're trying to assess the
effectiveness of Yahoo ads--

00:20:28.238 --> 00:20:30.405 align:middle line:84%
ambitious project trying
to assess the effectiveness

00:20:30.405 --> 00:20:35.220 align:middle line:84%
on Yahoo ads on both online
purchases and offline purchases,

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:38.070 align:middle line:84%
which is something that's
usually much harder to get at.

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:39.713 align:middle line:90%
So how did this experiment work?

00:20:39.713 --> 00:20:43.420 align:middle line:90%


00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:46.340 align:middle line:84%
So they run a randomized
controlled trial.

00:20:46.340 --> 00:20:50.080 align:middle line:84%
So they have some
large offline firm

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:52.692 align:middle line:84%
that's interested in running
a big ad campaign on Yahoo.

00:20:52.692 --> 00:20:54.400 align:middle line:84%
And they propose to
the firm that they're

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:55.870 align:middle line:84%
going to evaluate
the ad campaign

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:58.150 align:middle line:84%
as well as running the
ad campaign for them.

00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:02.770 align:middle line:84%
So the retailer-- so
think of this as a firm

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:06.100 align:middle line:84%
like Macy's or Bloomingdale's,
some very large department

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:08.800 align:middle line:90%
store, probably.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:11.590 align:middle line:84%
This large retailer
had a data-- had

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:14.830 align:middle line:84%
a data set of 1.5
million customers

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.770 align:middle line:84%
that they wanted
to advertise to.

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:19.120 align:middle line:84%
So they're going to display
the advertising only

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:20.710 align:middle line:90%
to those people.

00:21:20.710 --> 00:21:22.930 align:middle line:84%
And then what they
did is they matched it

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:26.560 align:middle line:90%
to the Yahoo database.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:30.370 align:middle line:84%
So you know, like today, Google
and Facebook and whomever

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:32.460 align:middle line:84%
know who you are when
you're searching.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.300 align:middle line:90%
People had Yahoo Mail accounts.

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:36.890 align:middle line:84%
They were permanently
logged into Yahoo.

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:39.880 align:middle line:84%
So they got someone
to match this database

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:42.500 align:middle line:84%
that the firm had of people
and their credit card

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:47.210 align:middle line:84%
numbers and their home addresses
to Yahoo's membership database.

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:51.740 align:middle line:84%
And so they were able to jointly
identify 1.5 million people who

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:55.383 align:middle line:84%
both existed in the retailer
database and had Yahoo accounts

00:21:55.383 --> 00:21:57.050 align:middle line:84%
that they may have
been logged into when

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:59.540 align:middle line:90%
they were web browsing.

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:03.600 align:middle line:84%
They used an 80/20 split
of treatment and control.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:06.950 align:middle line:84%
So 80% of the people, they
tried to advertise to them

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:08.030 align:middle line:90%
when they saw them.

00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:10.310 align:middle line:84%
20% of the people they
held out as controls

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:11.935 align:middle line:84%
and they never
advertised to them.

00:22:11.935 --> 00:22:14.990 align:middle line:90%


00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:18.050 align:middle line:84%
One thing about the treatment
control design like this is,

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:21.740 align:middle line:84%
again, the retailer
had a bid that it

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:24.620 align:middle line:84%
was willing to pay to
reach those people.

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:28.640 align:middle line:84%
And so I would guess, early
2000s advertising prices,

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:32.690 align:middle line:84%
it may have been, like, $1 per
1,000 or something like that

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:34.470 align:middle line:90%
for impression ads.

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:36.305 align:middle line:90%
So that every time--

00:22:36.305 --> 00:22:39.620 align:middle line:84%
every time one of these ads
was displayed to a consumer,

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:42.890 align:middle line:90%
they owed Yahoo 1/10 of $0.01.

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:46.820 align:middle line:84%
Whether those ads actually got
displayed depended on what else

00:22:46.820 --> 00:22:47.570 align:middle line:90%
that person--

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.153 align:middle line:84%
whether that person was actually
on the internet and what else

00:22:50.153 --> 00:22:50.850 align:middle line:90%
they were doing.

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:53.060 align:middle line:84%
So if that person was
on the internet, only

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:55.063 align:middle line:90%
rarely they might not see an ad.

00:22:55.063 --> 00:22:56.480 align:middle line:84%
If that person was
on the internet

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.270 align:middle line:84%
but they'd recently looked at
shoes or something like that

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:01.950 align:middle line:84%
and had a re-targeting ad that
wanted to follow them around,

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:04.790 align:middle line:84%
that re-targeting ad
would outbid the Macy's ad

00:23:04.790 --> 00:23:06.475 align:middle line:84%
and they would never
see the Macy's ad.

00:23:06.475 --> 00:23:07.850 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be if
that person was

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:09.840 align:middle line:84%
in a very valuable
demographic group,

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:13.290 align:middle line:84%
somebody wanted young people,
the young people price

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:16.210 align:middle line:84%
would be higher than
Macy's bid also.

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:20.080 align:middle line:84%
So whether or not you
see an ad, you know, it's

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:21.330 align:middle line:90%
like an intent to treat thing.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:23.730 align:middle line:84%
Like, being in the
treatment group

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:27.990 align:middle line:84%
means Macy's is bidding to get
your their ad shown to you,

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:32.235 align:middle line:84%
but they may not actually
get their ad shown to you.

00:23:32.235 --> 00:23:36.840 align:middle line:84%
And then the impressive part
of this campaign was they're

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:39.630 align:middle line:84%
able to monitor the
purchases by these people

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:41.100 align:middle line:90%
through the online--

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:45.030 align:middle line:84%
so this store is a store
that has an online presence,

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:47.580 align:middle line:84%
but they have much
bigger offline sales.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:52.440 align:middle line:84%
And they will monitor purchases
both online and offline

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:53.740 align:middle line:90%
by those consumers.

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:56.250 align:middle line:84%
So if somebody who
saw one of these--

00:23:56.250 --> 00:23:59.310 align:middle line:84%
a Macy's ad, walks
into the Macy's store,

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:00.163 align:middle line:90%
they buy something.

00:24:00.163 --> 00:24:01.830 align:middle line:84%
If they use their
credit card, Macy's is

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:03.010 align:middle line:90%
going to know who they are.

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:04.350 align:middle line:84%
They're going to
record all those sales,

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:05.940 align:middle line:84%
and they match
those sales back up

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:09.215 align:middle line:84%
to what you saw on the
internet five days earlier.

00:24:09.215 --> 00:24:17.730 align:middle line:90%


00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:22.320 align:middle line:84%
So what if things-- campaigns
looked through like this?

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:25.830 align:middle line:84%
You know, again, you're putting
those relatively large ads

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:26.720 align:middle line:90%
on there.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:28.560 align:middle line:90%
I don't know what the ads said.

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:33.180 align:middle line:84%
The ads could have said,
Macy's sale, 15% off all

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:36.360 align:middle line:84%
purchases with this code,
something like that.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:39.900 align:middle line:84%
The ad got a 0.28%
click-through rate.

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:43.580 align:middle line:84%
So when people were displayed
an ad, there was a probability--

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:46.350 align:middle line:90%
you know, 28 per thousand--

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:49.530 align:middle line:84%
28 per 10,000 that
they would click on it.

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:56.730 align:middle line:84%
7.2% of those who saw at
least one ad clicked on an ad

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:58.650 align:middle line:84%
during the course
of the campaign.

00:24:58.650 --> 00:25:01.120 align:middle line:84%
So they were advertising
people many, many times.

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:03.130 align:middle line:84%
So you can get a sense
of the scale here.

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.590 align:middle line:84%
So there were two
waves of the campaign.

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:07.170 align:middle line:84%
There was an early
fall campaign.

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:09.280 align:middle line:84%
Then there was a
late fall campaign.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:12.360 align:middle line:84%
The early fall campaign
was the main one.

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:19.280 align:middle line:84%
So they displayed 32 million
ads to 814,000 people.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:23.240 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember, the intent
to treat was 1.2 million.

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:26.710 align:middle line:90%
So 2/3 of the people--

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:30.580 align:middle line:84%
1/3 of the people either never
got on the internet or got

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:34.360 align:middle line:84%
on the internet only rarely and
Macy's never made it to the top

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:37.430 align:middle line:84%
of their bid queue to
display an ad to them.

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:40.360 align:middle line:84%
But the people who
were on the internet,

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:43.030 align:middle line:84%
they saw an average-- if you
conditional on seeing at least

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:46.900 align:middle line:84%
one ad, you saw an
average of 39.6 ads.

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:52.180 align:middle line:84%
And then conditional on
seeing at least one ad, 7%

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:54.790 align:middle line:84%
chance that you clicked
on an ad at some point

00:25:54.790 --> 00:25:57.960 align:middle line:90%
during the campaign.

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.210 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
campaign was a bit smaller.

00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:02.610 align:middle line:84%
You can see it's about
1/3 ads per viewer.

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:03.980 align:middle line:90%
13%.

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:08.510 align:middle line:84%
Reach was only slightly
less, 56 versus 63.

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:13.250 align:middle line:84%
And you'll notice the
mean ad views per viewer

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:14.690 align:middle line:90%
isn't 39 plus 13.

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:16.190 align:middle line:84%
It's a little bit
less because there

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.440 align:middle line:84%
are some people who only saw
an ad in the first campaign

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:20.857 align:middle line:84%
and some people who only saw
an ad in the second campaign.

00:26:20.857 --> 00:26:25.290 align:middle line:90%


00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:27.840 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what are my observations?

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:30.270 align:middle line:90%
The first one is actually--

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:33.180 align:middle line:84%
sometimes you'd write a paper,
and the paper kind of fails.

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:35.220 align:middle line:84%
And then in some sense
they resurrect the paper.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:37.590 align:middle line:84%
It's like, the
failure of this paper

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:40.840 align:middle line:84%
is notable that, like, we
get very imprecise estimates.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:42.870 align:middle line:84%
And part of their
thing was that us

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:44.490 align:middle line:84%
going into this maybe
as naive people,

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:48.000 align:middle line:84%
we thought, we have
this massive experiment,

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:50.250 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to
target a million people

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:51.878 align:middle line:90%
and send 30 million ads at them.

00:26:51.878 --> 00:26:53.670 align:middle line:84%
We will be able to get
very precise returns

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:54.820 align:middle line:90%
of the [INAUDIBLE] advertising.

00:26:54.820 --> 00:26:56.195 align:middle line:84%
And one of the
first observations

00:26:56.195 --> 00:26:58.740 align:middle line:84%
is that estimating
return on advertising

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:02.130 align:middle line:84%
is really, really hard,
and it's really hard even

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:05.415 align:middle line:84%
with advertising-- with
campaigns of this scale.

00:27:05.415 --> 00:27:06.260 align:middle line:90%
And so why is that?

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:07.750 align:middle line:90%
Here's a simple calculation.

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:10.230 align:middle line:84%
So first thing
about the sales data

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:12.790 align:middle line:84%
is that sales are
very, very noisy.

00:27:12.790 --> 00:27:15.640 align:middle line:84%
If you think about-- like,
what does Macy's care about?

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:18.150 align:middle line:84%
I don't know that the store is
Macy's, but just a store like

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:18.703 align:middle line:90%
Macy's.

00:27:18.703 --> 00:27:19.870 align:middle line:90%
What does Macy's care about?

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:21.820 align:middle line:84%
It's how much people
buy from them,

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:24.970 align:middle line:84%
and so they care about the
dollar value of purchases.

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:28.690 align:middle line:84%
What do purchases from
Macy's look like in the data?

00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:33.570 align:middle line:84%
It's an awful lot of zeros
and then a few $100 and $200

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:36.070 align:middle line:90%
and $500 and $1,000 things.

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:39.030 align:middle line:84%
So you get this enormous
variance in how much

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:43.950 align:middle line:84%
purchases people make from
Macy's in a given week.

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:48.640 align:middle line:84%
So their sales and
the treatment period

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:53.890 align:middle line:84%
average $1.89, although
this is a fake currency

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:56.680 align:middle line:84%
to not reveal exactly how
much money was involved.

00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:59.650 align:middle line:84%
But they average
$1.89 and they have

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:02.360 align:middle line:90%
a standard deviation of $19.

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:06.820 align:middle line:84%
So the standard deviation of
sales to a customer in a week

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:08.950 align:middle line:90%
or whatever is--

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:13.180 align:middle line:84%
actually, in a two-week
period is 10 times--

00:28:13.180 --> 00:28:15.400 align:middle line:84%
standard deviation
is 10 times the mean

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:17.030 align:middle line:84%
because you just
have a lot of zeros

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:20.530 align:middle line:90%
and then this fat upper tail.

00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:22.630 align:middle line:84%
A percent of return
on investment

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:25.010 align:middle line:84%
is a very small
fraction of sales.

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:30.550 align:middle line:84%
So this campaign cost
about 1% of sales

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:31.956 align:middle line:90%
to the targeted customers.

00:28:31.956 --> 00:28:34.570 align:middle line:90%


00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:38.800 align:middle line:84%
So that says that
they were paying--

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:40.510 align:middle line:90%
if they were averaging--

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:44.950 align:middle line:84%
if they were averaging $2
worth of sales per person,

00:28:44.950 --> 00:28:55.150 align:middle line:84%
they were only averaging
spending $0.02 on this campaign.

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:57.540 align:middle line:90%
So if you think about the way--

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:58.680 align:middle line:90%
like, if you're selling--

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:00.180 align:middle line:84%
if you have a
campaign, the campaign

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:02.850 align:middle line:84%
is costing you 1% of
your sales, suppose

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:10.050 align:middle line:84%
your profit margins are 50%
Then if your sales go up by 2%--

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:13.450 align:middle line:84%
if your sales go up by 2%,
then your profit goes up by 1%.

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:16.300 align:middle line:84%
That offsets the cost of
the advertising campaign.

00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:19.990 align:middle line:84%
So if sales go up by
2%, they're break-even.

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:24.460 align:middle line:84%
If sales go up by 2.2%, that's
a 10% return on the investment.

00:29:24.460 --> 00:29:29.550 align:middle line:84%
If sales go up by 1.8%,
that's a negative 10% return

00:29:29.550 --> 00:29:30.760 align:middle line:90%
on the investment.

00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:34.680 align:middle line:84%
So the return on the
investment, what you need to do

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:38.820 align:middle line:84%
is be able to estimate
whether, did sales go up by--

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:44.730 align:middle line:84%
you need a-- did sales-- you
need a standard error of 0.1%

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:47.970 align:middle line:84%
on your estimate of the effect
on sales to be able to tell--

00:29:47.970 --> 00:29:52.260 align:middle line:84%
if you're trying to tell
2% apart from 2.2% to 1.8%,

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:55.930 align:middle line:84%
you want a standard
error of 0.1%.

00:29:55.930 --> 00:30:01.493 align:middle line:84%
So you need a standard error of
your estimate of 1/1000 of mean

00:30:01.493 --> 00:30:01.993 align:middle line:90%
sales.

00:30:01.993 --> 00:30:06.260 align:middle line:90%


00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:10.310 align:middle line:84%
So then since the standard
deviation is 10 times the mean,

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:13.150 align:middle line:84%
that's 1/10,000 of a
standard deviation.

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:15.050 align:middle line:84%
If you think of
variances going down

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:19.790 align:middle line:84%
like root n, that's
like 1 over 100 million

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:22.280 align:middle line:84%
to get the standard
error you want.

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:26.390 align:middle line:84%
And so even though this campaign
seems like this campaign had--

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:30.440 align:middle line:84%
whether you think of it as a
million observations of people

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:34.753 align:middle line:84%
or you think of it as 30 million
observations of ads, orders

00:30:34.753 --> 00:30:36.170 align:middle line:84%
of magnitude,
you're going to need

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:38.540 align:middle line:84%
tens of millions or
hundreds of millions

00:30:38.540 --> 00:30:41.090 align:middle line:84%
to do something like this
and get statistically

00:30:41.090 --> 00:30:42.500 align:middle line:90%
significant estimates.

00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:45.650 align:middle line:84%
So it's a cautionary
tale that we

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:50.330 align:middle line:84%
should think of estimating
returns to advertising for goods

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:53.660 align:middle line:84%
like this where there's an upper
tail of sales as a very, very

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:54.685 align:middle line:90%
difficult thing to do.

00:30:54.685 --> 00:31:00.630 align:middle line:90%


00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:01.410 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:05.700 align:middle line:84%
Second observation they
make is that this is really

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:08.220 align:middle line:84%
another cautionary
tale for many papers

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:13.260 align:middle line:84%
you see that are estimating
advertising, that--

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:17.230 align:middle line:84%
you know, viewing advertising
is not an exogenous treatment.

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:22.745 align:middle line:84%
You can't just regress purchases
on whether someone saw an ad.

00:31:22.745 --> 00:31:24.370 align:middle line:84%
Here, they have
experimental variation.

00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:26.745 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose you didn't have
the experimental variation.

00:31:26.745 --> 00:31:30.105 align:middle line:84%
You try to regress purchases
on whether people saw an ad.

00:31:30.105 --> 00:31:32.580 align:middle line:84%
That's not going to be
a very good estimate.

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:34.775 align:middle line:84%
So there's this--
many firms like--

00:31:34.775 --> 00:31:36.900 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you've ever
thought it seems weird.

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:38.545 align:middle line:84%
Like, you do a
Google search and you

00:31:38.545 --> 00:31:40.170 align:middle line:84%
know you want to rent
a car from Hertz.

00:31:40.170 --> 00:31:42.030 align:middle line:84%
And you type in
Hertz, and then you

00:31:42.030 --> 00:31:44.680 align:middle line:84%
get-- the first thing
is an ad for hertz.com,

00:31:44.680 --> 00:31:47.020 align:middle line:84%
and the second thing
is just hertz.com.

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:50.093 align:middle line:84%
And you know, Hertz is paying
$0.50 per click for that top ad.

00:31:50.093 --> 00:31:51.510 align:middle line:84%
And you click on
that first click,

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:55.440 align:middle line:84%
and you're wondering, like, why
does Hertz buy that top ad that

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:56.500 align:middle line:90%
says hertz.com?

00:31:56.500 --> 00:31:58.665 align:middle line:84%
You know, it does make--
it does bump Avis down

00:31:58.665 --> 00:32:00.790 align:middle line:84%
to third on the page instead
of second on the page,

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:01.748 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:32:01.748 --> 00:32:05.080 align:middle line:84%
But you know, again, if you
do a naive study to Hertz

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:07.600 align:middle line:84%
and say, oh, you bought
this ad campaign on Hertz,

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:10.660 align:middle line:84%
and of the people who clicked
on your ad for hertz.com,

00:32:10.660 --> 00:32:13.280 align:middle line:84%
you know, 30% of them went
and rented a car from you.

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:17.000 align:middle line:84%
You did fabulously well on your
$0.50 that you paid in the fee.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:19.510 align:middle line:84%
Well, obviously, those people
were clicking on the Hertz ad

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:21.010 align:middle line:84%
because they'd
already done a search

00:32:21.010 --> 00:32:22.520 align:middle line:90%
and typed in the word hertz.com.

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:24.590 align:middle line:84%
They were going to
buy from you anyway.

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:27.970 align:middle line:84%
And so the fact that someone
sees your ad, in that case

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:30.985 align:middle line:84%
it's obvious, or clicks
on your ad, that tells you

00:32:30.985 --> 00:32:32.610 align:middle line:84%
an awful lot about
the person's intent,

00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:34.780 align:middle line:84%
and you can't view
that ad as exogenous.

00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:39.250 align:middle line:84%
Here, they're arguing
that this is true.

00:32:39.250 --> 00:32:43.900 align:middle line:84%
You know, even in an environment
like their experiment where--

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:49.600 align:middle line:84%
you know, whether someone sees
the ad for Macy's is different--

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:52.840 align:middle line:84%
is determined by whether they're
on the internet to begin with,

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:54.890 align:middle line:84%
whether by their
browsing behavior.

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:57.205 align:middle line:84%
If you had just done a
naive estimate of someone--

00:32:57.205 --> 00:32:59.080 align:middle line:84%
like, you think about
it, you do an estimate.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:01.863 align:middle line:84%
Even if you don't have this data
set, you do an estimate of--

00:33:01.863 --> 00:33:02.780 align:middle line:90%
not the Hertz example.

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:06.160 align:middle line:84%
Just when someone sees
an ad for Hertz cars,

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:09.008 align:middle line:84%
are they more likely to
rent a Hertz car than not?

00:33:09.008 --> 00:33:11.050 align:middle line:84%
Yes, they're more likely
to rent a Hertz car when

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:13.900 align:middle line:84%
they see a Hertz rectangle ad
just pop up on their screen

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:16.220 align:middle line:90%
because they're on the internet.

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:18.370 align:middle line:84%
And if they're out
hiking with their friends

00:33:18.370 --> 00:33:20.770 align:middle line:84%
or if they're in a movie
theater or they're in a bar,

00:33:20.770 --> 00:33:22.390 align:middle line:84%
they're never going
to rent a Hertz

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:26.900 align:middle line:84%
car because they're not on their
computer or on their phone.

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:30.070 align:middle line:90%
And so browsing isn't random.

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:33.203 align:middle line:84%
And you can't think of, I
put individual fixed effects

00:33:33.203 --> 00:33:34.370 align:middle line:90%
and that solves the problem.

00:33:34.370 --> 00:33:37.400 align:middle line:84%
People are different people
at different points in time.

00:33:37.400 --> 00:33:39.850 align:middle line:84%
And there's you who's
the browsing internet you

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:42.190 align:middle line:84%
and there's you who's
doing something else you.

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:43.400 align:middle line:90%
Those are different people.

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:45.775 align:middle line:84%
They have different preferences
and different likelihoods

00:33:45.775 --> 00:33:46.840 align:middle line:90%
to purchase.

00:33:46.840 --> 00:33:49.990 align:middle line:84%
And then there's also
these ad-serving engines

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:51.580 align:middle line:90%
have all these ads available.

00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:54.460 align:middle line:84%
The untargeted ones, the content
ones, the demographic ones,

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:55.970 align:middle line:90%
the re-targeting ones.

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:58.310 align:middle line:84%
All of those things can
affect your ad views.

00:33:58.310 --> 00:34:01.717 align:middle line:84%
So whether you see an ad or
not depends on who you are,

00:34:01.717 --> 00:34:04.300 align:middle line:84%
and it's going to be correlated
with your likelihood of buying

00:34:04.300 --> 00:34:08.199 align:middle line:84%
something from Macy's because
people who buy from Macy's just

00:34:08.199 --> 00:34:10.120 align:middle line:90%
are different than other people.

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:13.060 align:middle line:84%
Usually, this makes us
overestimate ad effectiveness.

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:14.860 align:middle line:84%
Here, what's funny
is that this is

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:16.690 align:middle line:84%
an example where this
was a campaign where

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:18.530 align:middle line:90%
the opposite effect was true.

00:34:18.530 --> 00:34:21.320 align:middle line:84%
That effect would make you
underestimate ad effectiveness.

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:24.980 align:middle line:84%
And so that's why I use Macy's
or Bloomingdale's as examples.

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:29.260 align:middle line:84%
My model for what's going on
here is you have a store that

00:34:29.260 --> 00:34:33.639 align:middle line:84%
sells to people who are in
their 60s or 70s who are not

00:34:33.639 --> 00:34:35.060 align:middle line:90%
on the internet much.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:37.540 align:middle line:84%
And then if you
learn that someone

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:39.760 align:middle line:84%
is on the internet
for hours a day,

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:42.639 align:middle line:84%
you learn that they're not
the typical customer of--

00:34:42.639 --> 00:34:44.350 align:middle line:84%
they're not the
high-spending customers

00:34:44.350 --> 00:34:45.590 align:middle line:90%
of that department store.

00:34:45.590 --> 00:34:47.590 align:middle line:84%
They're people who
are younger people who

00:34:47.590 --> 00:34:51.219 align:middle line:84%
may have one time bought
something at Macy's, but rarely

00:34:51.219 --> 00:34:54.250 align:middle line:84%
shop at large, fancy
department stores.

00:34:54.250 --> 00:34:58.120 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the fact that
you saw an ad-- like,

00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:01.510 align:middle line:84%
seeing an ad in
the-- because they

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:04.630 align:middle line:84%
have sales in the pre-period
before the campaign started.

00:35:04.630 --> 00:35:07.720 align:middle line:84%
And so what they find is
that the people who saw ads

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:09.970 align:middle line:84%
were buying less in the
pre-period than the people who

00:35:09.970 --> 00:35:12.370 align:middle line:90%
didn't see ads.

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:14.740 align:middle line:84%
So here, there's a
negative correlation.

00:35:14.740 --> 00:35:20.950 align:middle line:84%
So I guess the difference was,
you know, $2 versus $1.81.

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:24.480 align:middle line:84%
And it's just-- the
viewers of the ads

00:35:24.480 --> 00:35:28.200 align:middle line:84%
are just a worse clientele than
the nonviewers of the ads based

00:35:28.200 --> 00:35:30.630 align:middle line:84%
on probably the demographics
of who are viewers

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:31.670 align:middle line:90%
and who are nonviewers.

00:35:31.670 --> 00:35:36.190 align:middle line:90%


00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:38.140 align:middle line:84%
And this is a
retailer where I think

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:42.550 align:middle line:84%
it was 85% of the sales in this
campaign were in-store rather

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:43.720 align:middle line:90%
than online.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:46.150 align:middle line:84%
So some people buy
from macys.com,

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:48.325 align:middle line:90%
but probably most people--

00:35:48.325 --> 00:35:50.220 align:middle line:84%
store sales dominate
their online sales.

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:53.410 align:middle line:90%


00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:54.691 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:35:54.691 --> 00:35:59.330 align:middle line:84%
As I said, intent to treat
estimates are inconclusive.

00:35:59.330 --> 00:36:05.290 align:middle line:84%
So you do have this sort of
very classic, high-quality IV

00:36:05.290 --> 00:36:09.550 align:middle line:84%
thing where you just instrument
for seeing an ad with being

00:36:09.550 --> 00:36:10.940 align:middle line:90%
in the treatment group.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:14.940 align:middle line:84%
And then you look at,
how do the sales compare?

00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:17.380 align:middle line:84%
That gives you a valid estimate
that's not contaminated

00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:18.850 align:middle line:90%
by any of these things.

00:36:18.850 --> 00:36:23.230 align:middle line:84%
And what you find, in
the treatment group--

00:36:23.230 --> 00:36:27.063 align:middle line:84%
in the treatment
group, sales are--

00:36:27.063 --> 00:36:28.480 align:middle line:84%
during the treatment
period, sales

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:30.880 align:middle line:84%
are $1.89 in the
treatment group,

00:36:30.880 --> 00:36:33.770 align:middle line:90%
$1.84 in the control group.

00:36:33.770 --> 00:36:39.610 align:middle line:84%
So the difference is 5.3 cents,
standard error of 3.8 cents.

00:36:39.610 --> 00:36:42.432 align:middle line:84%
In terms of estimating
the ROI of this campaign,

00:36:42.432 --> 00:36:44.140 align:middle line:84%
they're estimating
that this campaign has

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:47.095 align:middle line:90%
a return on investment of 40%.

00:36:47.095 --> 00:36:49.640 align:middle line:84%
So Macy's should be very
happy to run this campaign.

00:36:49.640 --> 00:36:53.910 align:middle line:84%
However, it's got a return
of 40% plus or minus 100%.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:57.140 align:middle line:84%
So we get this very, very
noisy estimate of the return

00:36:57.140 --> 00:36:59.640 align:middle line:84%
to the campaign, suggesting
that this campaign is effective,

00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:03.500 align:middle line:90%
but we really don't know.

00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:06.140 align:middle line:84%
They then also do a
differences-in-differences

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:10.460 align:middle line:84%
equation, taking into account
that the pre-period treatment

00:37:10.460 --> 00:37:14.510 align:middle line:84%
control group purchases
were not exactly identical.

00:37:14.510 --> 00:37:17.870 align:middle line:84%
You were randomizing
1.5 million people,

00:37:17.870 --> 00:37:20.660 align:middle line:84%
but when you randomize
1.5 million people,

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:24.530 align:middle line:84%
they're not always exactly
the same representative.

00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:28.165 align:middle line:84%
And they didn't randomize
based on pre-period purchases

00:37:28.165 --> 00:37:29.540 align:middle line:84%
because they didn't
have the data

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:30.500 align:middle line:84%
on pre-period purchase
at the time they

00:37:30.500 --> 00:37:32.270 align:middle line:90%
were doing the randomization.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:34.850 align:middle line:84%
So anyway,
differences-in-differences

00:37:34.850 --> 00:37:39.540 align:middle line:84%
account estimate that it's
8.3 cents plus or minus 5.9.

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:44.390 align:middle line:84%
So even bigger return on
investment, even noisier

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:45.240 align:middle line:90%
estimate.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:49.760 align:middle line:90%


00:37:49.760 --> 00:37:51.380 align:middle line:84%
Difference-in-difference
estimates

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:53.900 align:middle line:84%
also suggest that there
may be long-run benefits

00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:55.460 align:middle line:90%
after the campaign ends.

00:37:55.460 --> 00:37:59.190 align:middle line:84%
The point estimates are you're
making 5-point-something cents

00:37:59.190 --> 00:37:59.690 align:middle line:90%
during--

00:37:59.690 --> 00:38:02.870 align:middle line:84%
5-point-something cents
during the treatment period.

00:38:02.870 --> 00:38:07.130 align:middle line:84%
You're making another $0.06 in
the week after and then $0.07

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:09.740 align:middle line:90%
total for the three weeks after.

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:11.420 align:middle line:84%
You can certainly
imagine that if this

00:38:11.420 --> 00:38:13.400 align:middle line:84%
is-- you're showing ads
for a department store,

00:38:13.400 --> 00:38:15.442 align:middle line:84%
some people see the ads
for the department store.

00:38:15.442 --> 00:38:16.940 align:middle line:84%
They decide-- that
makes them think

00:38:16.940 --> 00:38:18.440 align:middle line:84%
about going to the
department store,

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:21.320 align:middle line:84%
and then they go the week after
they've seen the ad online

00:38:21.320 --> 00:38:27.470 align:middle line:84%
or they've seen 30 ads online,
or something like that.

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:32.023 align:middle line:84%
The paper referred
to the sort of

00:38:32.023 --> 00:38:33.440 align:middle line:84%
difference-in-difference
estimates

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:36.110 align:middle line:90%
as their preferred estimates.

00:38:36.110 --> 00:38:38.412 align:middle line:84%
I mean, my view is I think
the difference-in-difference

00:38:38.412 --> 00:38:39.870 align:middle line:84%
estimates are
somewhat problematic.

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:41.537 align:middle line:84%
Again, if you take
this view that people

00:38:41.537 --> 00:38:44.240 align:middle line:84%
are different people
on different days,

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:47.210 align:middle line:84%
do we really want to trust
putting in individual fixed

00:38:47.210 --> 00:38:47.710 align:middle line:90%
effects?

00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:52.830 align:middle line:84%
Because maybe the-- maybe the
reason that people spent less

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:55.200 align:middle line:84%
in the pre-period in
one group than the other

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:57.330 align:middle line:84%
was because during
the pre-period

00:38:57.330 --> 00:39:00.240 align:middle line:84%
they were doing things other
than shopping and postponing

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:00.880 align:middle line:90%
their shopping.

00:39:00.880 --> 00:39:03.390 align:middle line:84%
And then during the next period,
they may be doing, like--

00:39:03.390 --> 00:39:05.500 align:middle line:84%
you know, there must be
something different about them.

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:07.500 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that they were
doing less shopping in one

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:08.790 align:middle line:84%
doesn't mean that they're
going to be doing less

00:39:08.790 --> 00:39:11.250 align:middle line:84%
shopping in another or they're
spending more time online,

00:39:11.250 --> 00:39:12.840 align:middle line:90%
one versus the other.

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:16.397 align:middle line:84%
I find that in some sense
the paper is very skeptical

00:39:16.397 --> 00:39:18.480 align:middle line:84%
that individual fixed
effects are a way to control

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:19.445 align:middle line:90%
for things like this.

00:39:19.445 --> 00:39:24.800 align:middle line:90%


00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:26.580 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that?

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:28.280 align:middle line:90%
And let me say, if you--

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:30.740 align:middle line:84%
are there other ad effectiveness
studies that give more

00:39:30.740 --> 00:39:31.940 align:middle line:90%
significant estimates?

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:33.440 align:middle line:90%
Yes, there are.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:38.500 align:middle line:84%
You know, one thing that's
difficult here is this--

00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:40.250 align:middle line:84%
you get that factor
of 10 that the mean is

00:39:40.250 --> 00:39:44.090 align:middle line:84%
$1.89 and the standard
deviation is $19.

00:39:44.090 --> 00:39:46.940 align:middle line:84%
If you're doing a zero-one
treatment where you're trying

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:49.640 align:middle line:84%
to get people to sign up
to buy cell phone service,

00:39:49.640 --> 00:39:52.280 align:middle line:84%
you show them an ad, they can
click on it and buy the cell

00:39:52.280 --> 00:39:55.952 align:middle line:84%
phone service right there, at
least that's one thing that--

00:39:55.952 --> 00:39:57.410 align:middle line:84%
you've eliminated
at least that one

00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:01.040 align:middle line:84%
source of the standard deviation
being 10 times the mean.

00:40:01.040 --> 00:40:04.190 align:middle line:90%
And so you can--

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:07.688 align:middle line:84%
at least some part
of it you can--

00:40:07.688 --> 00:40:09.230 align:middle line:84%
some part of it you
can overcome when

00:40:09.230 --> 00:40:13.187 align:middle line:84%
you have the low variance
in the individual-level data

00:40:13.187 --> 00:40:15.770 align:middle line:84%
rather than the high variance
in the individual-level purchase

00:40:15.770 --> 00:40:16.270 align:middle line:90%
data.

00:40:16.270 --> 00:40:19.140 align:middle line:90%


00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:19.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:19.640 --> 00:40:21.432 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, you can also
estimate ROI better

00:40:21.432 --> 00:40:23.630 align:middle line:84%
if the campaign
is more expensive.

00:40:23.630 --> 00:40:28.386 align:middle line:84%
If you have someone who's
basically spending all of it--

00:40:28.386 --> 00:40:30.080 align:middle line:84%
you know, this
advertising campaign

00:40:30.080 --> 00:40:33.118 align:middle line:84%
is scaled to be a small fraction
of the revenue of this store.

00:40:33.118 --> 00:40:35.660 align:middle line:84%
If you have a campaign that's
a large fraction of the revenue

00:40:35.660 --> 00:40:37.940 align:middle line:84%
of some online
business, that, again,

00:40:37.940 --> 00:40:40.523 align:middle line:84%
makes it easier to
estimate the return.

00:40:40.523 --> 00:40:55.050 align:middle line:90%


00:40:55.050 --> 00:40:55.860 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:40:55.860 --> 00:40:57.510 align:middle line:90%
So question?

00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:01.260 align:middle line:84%
I'll go on-- I'm going to do
another difference-in-difference

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:02.130 align:middle line:90%
paper.

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:06.480 align:middle line:84%
And I wanted to include
Tobias's paper, both

00:41:06.480 --> 00:41:08.970 align:middle line:84%
because it's been great
to have him in the course,

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:12.906 align:middle line:84%
and because I think it's
a really nice paper.

00:41:12.906 --> 00:41:17.040 align:middle line:84%
And I think actually,
this is evidence

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:20.490 align:middle line:84%
on what I think of as a very,
very timely and important

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:23.970 align:middle line:90%
question in online advertising.

00:41:23.970 --> 00:41:29.040 align:middle line:84%
You know, in online
advertising, targeting is just

00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:31.560 align:middle line:90%
playing a tremendous role now.

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:33.300 align:middle line:84%
Most ads that you
see on the internet

00:41:33.300 --> 00:41:36.210 align:middle line:90%
are targeted somehow to you.

00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:40.200 align:middle line:84%
Targeted ads sell
for much higher rates

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:42.210 align:middle line:90%
than untargeted ads.

00:41:42.210 --> 00:41:45.640 align:middle line:90%
You know, it can be a--

00:41:45.640 --> 00:41:48.480 align:middle line:84%
you know, order of magnitude
or two orders of magnitude

00:41:48.480 --> 00:41:49.890 align:middle line:90%
difference in prices.

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:54.700 align:middle line:84%
Just if you have just a random
web page and someone's using

00:41:54.700 --> 00:41:57.080 align:middle line:84%
privacy blocking software,
you know nothing about them,

00:41:57.080 --> 00:41:59.830 align:middle line:84%
you're just trying to show
them a display ad for Coca-Cola

00:41:59.830 --> 00:42:02.740 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, those ads
cost very, very little.

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:05.140 align:middle line:84%
Those ads following you
around of the things that

00:42:05.140 --> 00:42:06.640 align:middle line:84%
are in your shopping
cart somewhere,

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:09.820 align:middle line:84%
those ads just cost, you
know, two orders of magnitude

00:42:09.820 --> 00:42:14.020 align:middle line:84%
more than the ad that's just
a random banner ad displayed

00:42:14.020 --> 00:42:19.210 align:middle line:84%
to anyone on the-- every
single person on the internet.

00:42:19.210 --> 00:42:22.720 align:middle line:84%
You know, there's been
increasing interest in privacy,

00:42:22.720 --> 00:42:24.160 align:middle line:90%
I think driven by two things.

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:27.460 align:middle line:84%
One is there's a set of
consumers who we know care

00:42:27.460 --> 00:42:30.250 align:middle line:84%
very much about
privacy, or at least

00:42:30.250 --> 00:42:32.450 align:middle line:84%
say they care very
much about privacy.

00:42:32.450 --> 00:42:35.000 align:middle line:84%
It's always one of these
puzzles that people

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:37.000 align:middle line:84%
say they care a tremendous
amount about privacy,

00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:39.940 align:middle line:84%
but then they do things
to give away their privacy

00:42:39.940 --> 00:42:42.210 align:middle line:90%
for almost nothing.

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:44.237 align:middle line:90%
So there are many--

00:42:44.237 --> 00:42:45.820 align:middle line:84%
you probably know,
on your phone there

00:42:45.820 --> 00:42:49.255 align:middle line:84%
are many apps that track you and
will report back to the mother

00:42:49.255 --> 00:42:50.590 align:middle line:90%
ship everything about you.

00:42:50.590 --> 00:42:54.310 align:middle line:84%
Like, you install-- you have
a weather app on your phone

00:42:54.310 --> 00:42:57.860 align:middle line:84%
that you look at once every
month to look at the weather,

00:42:57.860 --> 00:43:00.890 align:middle line:84%
and that weather thing is
actually reporting your location

00:43:00.890 --> 00:43:05.800 align:middle line:84%
back to the owner of the
weather app every half hour

00:43:05.800 --> 00:43:08.770 align:middle line:84%
when your phone is active,
and sending that information

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:11.138 align:middle line:84%
to all kinds of advertisers
who are targeting you

00:43:11.138 --> 00:43:12.430 align:middle line:90%
based on knowing where you are.

00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:16.145 align:middle line:84%
And you gave up all that
privacy just to install some app

00:43:16.145 --> 00:43:18.520 align:middle line:84%
and then not bother to figure
out in its privacy settings

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:20.950 align:middle line:84%
whether it's allowed to
send your location to people

00:43:20.950 --> 00:43:24.010 align:middle line:90%
or not when you're not using it.

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:25.390 align:middle line:90%
People are often also--

00:43:25.390 --> 00:43:28.990 align:middle line:84%
you'll get some click on
here for enhanced displays,

00:43:28.990 --> 00:43:32.810 align:middle line:84%
or something like that,
and you get better service.

00:43:32.810 --> 00:43:35.230 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, people
say they don't want

00:43:35.230 --> 00:43:36.650 align:middle line:90%
to be tracked on the internet.

00:43:36.650 --> 00:43:40.480 align:middle line:84%
And then also, there's a battle
between different internet

00:43:40.480 --> 00:43:41.710 align:middle line:90%
firms.

00:43:41.710 --> 00:43:47.770 align:middle line:84%
So you know, Apple in particular
probably prefers for Google

00:43:47.770 --> 00:43:49.698 align:middle line:84%
to make less money
rather than more,

00:43:49.698 --> 00:43:52.240 align:middle line:84%
and Apple prefers for Facebook
to make less money rather than

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:55.270 align:middle line:84%
more, and Apple would like
Apple to make all the money.

00:43:55.270 --> 00:43:59.110 align:middle line:84%
Apple knows that these
other firms that may--

00:43:59.110 --> 00:44:03.490 align:middle line:84%
like, Google may be able to
sell Android phones really cheap

00:44:03.490 --> 00:44:06.080 align:middle line:84%
because Google makes all
the advertising revenue.

00:44:06.080 --> 00:44:09.010 align:middle line:84%
If Apple can get Google to
make less advertising revenue,

00:44:09.010 --> 00:44:11.470 align:middle line:84%
Google may be less able to
compete with Apple in the phone

00:44:11.470 --> 00:44:12.430 align:middle line:90%
space.

00:44:12.430 --> 00:44:14.980 align:middle line:84%
So Apple's been
a big proponent--

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:16.580 align:middle line:84%
maybe for the goodness
of their heart,

00:44:16.580 --> 00:44:19.120 align:middle line:84%
they've been a big
proponent of online privacy

00:44:19.120 --> 00:44:21.310 align:middle line:84%
and of settings
in the iPhone that

00:44:21.310 --> 00:44:24.520 align:middle line:84%
make it harder for
firms to track you using

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:26.560 align:middle line:90%
data that's on your iPhone.

00:44:26.560 --> 00:44:29.260 align:middle line:84%
So there's both
sort of Apple trying

00:44:29.260 --> 00:44:32.560 align:middle line:84%
to be the protector of privacy
and governments, especially

00:44:32.560 --> 00:44:35.170 align:middle line:84%
in Europe, trying to be the
protector of people's privacy

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:37.635 align:middle line:84%
and make it harder
to track people.

00:44:37.635 --> 00:44:40.315 align:middle line:84%
And you know, it's
a big question of,

00:44:40.315 --> 00:44:44.530 align:middle line:84%
what is going to happen if
it does get harder and harder

00:44:44.530 --> 00:44:47.110 align:middle line:84%
to track consumers
on the internet?

00:44:47.110 --> 00:44:50.540 align:middle line:84%
One is you could have a real
decline in ad-supported content.

00:44:50.540 --> 00:44:53.110 align:middle line:84%
It may be that websites-- you
know, obviously, The New York

00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:54.550 align:middle line:90%
Times does have a paywall.

00:44:54.550 --> 00:44:58.000 align:middle line:84%
But it could be that many online
newspapers that relied largely

00:44:58.000 --> 00:44:59.770 align:middle line:84%
on advertising, if
they can't track

00:44:59.770 --> 00:45:01.938 align:middle line:84%
the consumers and
their advertisements,

00:45:01.938 --> 00:45:03.730 align:middle line:84%
instead of being able
to show you the shoes

00:45:03.730 --> 00:45:06.400 align:middle line:84%
that you put in your shopping
cart, all they can do

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:08.300 align:middle line:90%
is show you an ad for Coca-Cola.

00:45:08.300 --> 00:45:11.560 align:middle line:84%
It may be they don't make enough
money to continue to survive,

00:45:11.560 --> 00:45:14.710 align:middle line:84%
and we may have a big decline
in ad-supported content

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:17.890 align:middle line:84%
relative to
non-ad-supported content.

00:45:17.890 --> 00:45:20.050 align:middle line:84%
There could be
twisting of what's

00:45:20.050 --> 00:45:21.290 align:middle line:90%
available on the internet.

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:24.790 align:middle line:84%
Like, you don't-- if
you know exactly--

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:27.640 align:middle line:84%
like, if you know that
someone is reading a,

00:45:27.640 --> 00:45:32.350 align:middle line:84%
you know, vintage
sneaker website well,

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:34.885 align:middle line:84%
you know what to advertise
to them if they're browsing

00:45:34.885 --> 00:45:36.250 align:middle line:90%
a vintage sneaker website.

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:40.060 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if they're just browsing
a news page or boston.com,

00:45:40.060 --> 00:45:42.020 align:middle line:84%
you have no idea what
to advertise to them.

00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:45.760 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that we get
this twisting of content

00:45:45.760 --> 00:45:48.632 align:middle line:84%
away from places that
need targeting ad

00:45:48.632 --> 00:45:50.590 align:middle line:84%
based on exogenous
information and the ones who

00:45:50.590 --> 00:45:54.110 align:middle line:84%
can target based on the
content of their page.

00:45:54.110 --> 00:45:55.790 align:middle line:84%
You could also
imagine this is going

00:45:55.790 --> 00:45:58.520 align:middle line:84%
to increase the power of
large versus small firms

00:45:58.520 --> 00:46:02.270 align:middle line:84%
because people are often
logged into Facebook

00:46:02.270 --> 00:46:05.340 align:middle line:84%
or logged into
Google all the time.

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:09.500 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, Google can advertise
to you in a targeted way,

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:11.300 align:middle line:84%
even if you're turning
off your cookies

00:46:11.300 --> 00:46:14.010 align:middle line:84%
for many other things because
you're still logged into Google.

00:46:14.010 --> 00:46:18.950 align:middle line:84%
And so it could be that blocking
other people from tracking then

00:46:18.950 --> 00:46:21.530 align:middle line:84%
just makes Google and
Facebook all the more dominant

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:23.613 align:middle line:84%
because they're the ones
that you're always logged

00:46:23.613 --> 00:46:26.360 align:middle line:84%
into and they can target
you when others can't.

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:29.120 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so most important
regulation on this thing

00:46:29.120 --> 00:46:34.290 align:middle line:84%
is EU's General Data
Protection Regulation, GDPR.

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:36.960 align:middle line:90%
It was adopted in April 2016.

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:39.565 align:middle line:90%
It went into effect May 2018.

00:46:39.565 --> 00:46:43.850 align:middle line:84%
And GDPR says that websites
cannot track European consumers

00:46:43.850 --> 00:46:48.110 align:middle line:90%
without explicit opt-in consent.

00:46:48.110 --> 00:46:50.160 align:middle line:84%
So I guess we don't have
many Europeans here.

00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:52.880 align:middle line:90%
So if you've--

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:56.250 align:middle line:84%
I mean, for me, I know it was a
shock going to Europe in 2018,

00:46:56.250 --> 00:46:59.040 align:middle line:84%
and just every single
website you went to--

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:01.650 align:middle line:84%
and when you had a slow home
internet connection, which

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:03.693 align:middle line:84%
I did, every website
you come, you're just

00:47:03.693 --> 00:47:04.860 align:middle line:90%
trying to look at a website.

00:47:04.860 --> 00:47:06.420 align:middle line:84%
And immediately
pops up this sort

00:47:06.420 --> 00:47:09.810 align:middle line:84%
of informed consent thing where
you have to give your consent

00:47:09.810 --> 00:47:13.710 align:middle line:84%
and click either yes, I
consent, or click on the show

00:47:13.710 --> 00:47:16.170 align:middle line:84%
me the privacy settings,
and then click on--

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:18.960 align:middle line:84%
if you show the privacy settings
and then click on the minimal,

00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:21.130 align:middle line:84%
automatically the default
is you get the minimal--

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:24.190 align:middle line:84%
only the strictly necessary
cookies are enabled.

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:27.210 align:middle line:84%
But every European website
has this pop-up thing

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:30.657 align:middle line:84%
that you have to answer the
very first time you visit it.

00:47:30.657 --> 00:47:31.740 align:middle line:90%
Maybe you're used to this.

00:47:31.740 --> 00:47:33.930 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you go to
look at any World Cup

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:36.690 align:middle line:84%
website or the guardian.com,
you always get this pop-up thing

00:47:36.690 --> 00:47:37.860 align:middle line:90%
that you have to get rid of.

00:47:37.860 --> 00:47:40.180 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, that's GDPR.

00:47:40.180 --> 00:47:44.430 align:middle line:84%
It went into effect
on one date, and not

00:47:44.430 --> 00:47:46.570 align:middle line:84%
everybody complied
on the first date.

00:47:46.570 --> 00:47:48.690 align:middle line:84%
But you know, there
were very large fines

00:47:48.690 --> 00:47:51.360 align:middle line:84%
for not complying with GDPR,
so many, many firms did

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:53.760 align:middle line:90%
comply pretty much right away.

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:57.950 align:middle line:84%
And then what Aridor, Che,
and Salz are trying to do

00:47:57.950 --> 00:48:00.950 align:middle line:84%
is use a
difference-in-difference design

00:48:00.950 --> 00:48:04.890 align:middle line:84%
to explore a number
of things about GDPR.

00:48:04.890 --> 00:48:08.480 align:middle line:84%
The first basic question
is, how are consumers

00:48:08.480 --> 00:48:11.512 align:middle line:84%
going to react to these
pop-up, opt-out things?

00:48:11.512 --> 00:48:13.970 align:middle line:84%
How many consumers are going
to opt out of data collection,

00:48:13.970 --> 00:48:15.512 align:middle line:84%
and how many consumers
are just going

00:48:15.512 --> 00:48:18.900 align:middle line:84%
to get used to clicking
yes, yes, yes, yes, yes?

00:48:18.900 --> 00:48:20.930 align:middle line:84%
Because it is the
single quickest way

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:27.230 align:middle line:84%
to get rid of the thing is
an accept all cookies box.

00:48:27.230 --> 00:48:29.540 align:middle line:84%
Second question is
then, how does GDPR

00:48:29.540 --> 00:48:33.030 align:middle line:84%
change the composition of
consumers who are observed?

00:48:33.030 --> 00:48:35.370 align:middle line:84%
You know, the consumers are
not opting out at random.

00:48:35.370 --> 00:48:38.330 align:middle line:84%
It's not like you're losing
x percent of consumers chosen

00:48:38.330 --> 00:48:40.950 align:middle line:84%
equally likely, You're losing
people who want to opt out,

00:48:40.950 --> 00:48:42.408 align:middle line:84%
and so the people
who opt out could

00:48:42.408 --> 00:48:45.320 align:middle line:84%
be different from the
firms that opt in.

00:48:45.320 --> 00:48:48.140 align:middle line:84%
And then in some sense,
it's one and two together

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:50.360 align:middle line:84%
that are determining
how GDPR is going

00:48:50.360 --> 00:48:53.690 align:middle line:84%
to impact firms that do rely on
consumer data for their business

00:48:53.690 --> 00:48:54.450 align:middle line:90%
model.

00:48:54.450 --> 00:48:56.100 align:middle line:84%
And they have this
interesting idea

00:48:56.100 --> 00:49:01.440 align:middle line:84%
that it's possible that
GDPR could even help firms

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:04.170 align:middle line:84%
because you're getting
rid of customers

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:05.980 align:middle line:90%
who don't want to be tracked.

00:49:05.980 --> 00:49:08.880 align:middle line:84%
It may be that customers who
don't want to be tracked just

00:49:08.880 --> 00:49:10.298 align:middle line:84%
naturally buy less,
or it could be

00:49:10.298 --> 00:49:12.090 align:middle line:84%
that consumers who
don't want to be tracked

00:49:12.090 --> 00:49:13.980 align:middle line:84%
are going to be
screwing with your data

00:49:13.980 --> 00:49:16.920 align:middle line:84%
by eliminating their cookies
or using pop-up blockers

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:18.250 align:middle line:90%
and using other things.

00:49:18.250 --> 00:49:19.980 align:middle line:84%
And once you get
those people who

00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:24.690 align:middle line:84%
are obfuscating their presence
and screwing up your statistics,

00:49:24.690 --> 00:49:26.310 align:middle line:84%
the statistics on
the remaining people

00:49:26.310 --> 00:49:28.540 align:middle line:84%
are cleaner and
easier to analyze.

00:49:28.540 --> 00:49:29.730 align:middle line:90%
And it could be that--

00:49:29.730 --> 00:49:31.380 align:middle line:84%
at least that could
offset part of what

00:49:31.380 --> 00:49:35.310 align:middle line:84%
you lose by not being able
to track people anymore.

00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:36.570 align:middle line:90%
So they have this--

00:49:36.570 --> 00:49:39.255 align:middle line:84%
again, it's a simple
difference-in-difference

00:49:39.255 --> 00:49:41.130 align:middle line:84%
estimate, but it's a
difference-in-difference

00:49:41.130 --> 00:49:45.570 align:middle line:84%
estimate exploiting, again, a
very detailed relationship with

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:46.770 align:middle line:90%
some firm that does--

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:48.670 align:middle line:90%
that operates online.

00:49:48.670 --> 00:49:51.240 align:middle line:84%
I had not been aware
that such a firm existed,

00:49:51.240 --> 00:49:55.950 align:middle line:84%
but here's my attempt to infer,
like, what does this firm do?

00:49:55.950 --> 00:50:00.240 align:middle line:84%
So this is an intermediary
that contracts, they say,

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:04.660 align:middle line:84%
with almost all top EU
and US travel sites.

00:50:04.660 --> 00:50:08.130 align:middle line:84%
So that means it's a very large
behind-the-scenes firm that

00:50:08.130 --> 00:50:11.370 align:middle line:84%
has contracts with pretty much
everybody, which I would not

00:50:11.370 --> 00:50:12.700 align:middle line:90%
have guessed existed.

00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:14.100 align:middle line:90%
And what does it do?

00:50:14.100 --> 00:50:16.560 align:middle line:84%
When you enter a query on
one of the sites-- like,

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:19.013 align:middle line:84%
I just went yesterday
to kayak.com

00:50:19.013 --> 00:50:20.430 align:middle line:84%
and typed in a
search for a flight

00:50:20.430 --> 00:50:22.213 align:middle line:90%
from Boston to Orange County.

00:50:22.213 --> 00:50:24.630 align:middle line:84%
And to try to look like somebody
who wasn't actually going

00:50:24.630 --> 00:50:27.460 align:middle line:84%
to buy the flight, I didn't
change the default dates.

00:50:27.460 --> 00:50:30.750 align:middle line:84%
I just clicked Boston to
Orange County, click Search,

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:33.518 align:middle line:84%
because I was trying to signal
to Kayak that I wasn't actually

00:50:33.518 --> 00:50:34.810 align:middle line:90%
going to buy this plane ticket.

00:50:34.810 --> 00:50:36.935 align:middle line:84%
And I figured that if
you ask, how is someone

00:50:36.935 --> 00:50:38.310 align:middle line:84%
going to not buy
the plane ticket

00:50:38.310 --> 00:50:41.250 align:middle line:84%
they just searched for, the
fact that they left the default

00:50:41.250 --> 00:50:43.620 align:middle line:84%
dates on there of
January 3 to January 10

00:50:43.620 --> 00:50:45.750 align:middle line:84%
is probably a good sign
that they're not buying.

00:50:45.750 --> 00:50:48.340 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, so what did Kayak do?

00:50:48.340 --> 00:50:49.980 align:middle line:90%
It popped up as--

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:51.780 align:middle line:84%
it did give me
the flight prices,

00:50:51.780 --> 00:50:56.500 align:middle line:84%
but it popped up as
the very first ad here

00:50:56.500 --> 00:50:58.083 align:middle line:90%
above all the flight prices.

00:50:58.083 --> 00:50:59.500 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
you can read this.

00:50:59.500 --> 00:51:01.630 align:middle line:90%
It says aa.com ad.

00:51:01.630 --> 00:51:07.120 align:middle line:84%
And this says, you know, Your
next adventure's a flight away,

00:51:07.120 --> 00:51:10.430 align:middle line:84%
$371 American Airlines,
quote, "View Deal."

00:51:10.430 --> 00:51:12.430 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to take
me away from the Kayak

00:51:12.430 --> 00:51:14.950 align:middle line:84%
website to the American
Airlines website

00:51:14.950 --> 00:51:17.320 align:middle line:84%
because the description
in the paper of what

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:19.660 align:middle line:84%
this intermediary
does is any time

00:51:19.660 --> 00:51:24.970 align:middle line:84%
anyone visits a travel website,
it has cookies on them.

00:51:24.970 --> 00:51:26.260 align:middle line:90%
It analyzes their behavior.

00:51:26.260 --> 00:51:27.760 align:middle line:84%
It does a machine
learning algorithm

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:29.218 align:middle line:84%
to figure out
whether they're going

00:51:29.218 --> 00:51:31.993 align:middle line:84%
to buy from the website
they're currently searching on.

00:51:31.993 --> 00:51:33.910 align:middle line:84%
If it thinks there's a
low probability they're

00:51:33.910 --> 00:51:36.370 align:middle line:84%
going to buy in the website
they're currently searching on,

00:51:36.370 --> 00:51:39.490 align:middle line:84%
it runs an auction and
auctions off the ability

00:51:39.490 --> 00:51:43.270 align:middle line:84%
to show a display ad to them,
to all the other firms that it

00:51:43.270 --> 00:51:44.150 align:middle line:90%
serves.

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:45.730 align:middle line:84%
And then those
other firms can bid

00:51:45.730 --> 00:51:48.160 align:middle line:84%
for this consumer who
is probably not going

00:51:48.160 --> 00:51:49.960 align:middle line:90%
to buy where they are now.

00:51:49.960 --> 00:51:52.000 align:middle line:84%
And so in this case-- so
what I think it did was

00:51:52.000 --> 00:51:54.380 align:middle line:84%
it said, Glenn is
not actually going

00:51:54.380 --> 00:51:56.960 align:middle line:84%
to purchase a ticket on
Kayak, so let's show him

00:51:56.960 --> 00:51:59.000 align:middle line:84%
an ad for something
he might want

00:51:59.000 --> 00:52:02.370 align:middle line:84%
to buy if he goes to the
American Airlines website.

00:52:02.370 --> 00:52:04.130 align:middle line:84%
American is the biggest
carrier, I think,

00:52:04.130 --> 00:52:05.760 align:middle line:90%
in Orange County airport.

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:07.520 align:middle line:90%
He may be trying to buy--

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:09.740 align:middle line:84%
it may be that
American Airlines is

00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:12.350 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay $0.09 to
have me visit their website

00:52:12.350 --> 00:52:15.530 align:middle line:90%
or something like that, OK?

00:52:15.530 --> 00:52:17.660 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so what
they have is they

00:52:17.660 --> 00:52:21.180 align:middle line:90%
have access to clickstream data.

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:24.920 align:middle line:84%
So they have, in some
sense, access to every--

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:33.250 align:middle line:84%
you know, Glenn Ellison typed
in at 10:13 PM last night,

00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:35.760 align:middle line:90%
search Boston-Santa Ana.

00:52:35.760 --> 00:52:37.530 align:middle line:90%
We conducted this auction.

00:52:37.530 --> 00:52:39.100 align:middle line:84%
American Airlines
won the auction.

00:52:39.100 --> 00:52:39.958 align:middle line:90%
They bid $0.09.

00:52:39.958 --> 00:52:42.250 align:middle line:84%
He either clicked on the
American website or he didn't.

00:52:42.250 --> 00:52:43.833 align:middle line:84%
If he clicked on the
American website,

00:52:43.833 --> 00:52:46.380 align:middle line:84%
did he buy from
American Airlines?

00:52:46.380 --> 00:52:49.470 align:middle line:84%
So they have the whole
clickstream of everything

00:52:49.470 --> 00:52:50.770 align:middle line:90%
that I was doing.

00:52:50.770 --> 00:52:55.180 align:middle line:84%
They mostly aggregated to the
website country week level,

00:52:55.180 --> 00:52:58.710 align:middle line:84%
so it's mostly going to be
comparing customers in France

00:52:58.710 --> 00:53:05.745 align:middle line:84%
on the first week of GDPR
on kayak.com to customers,

00:53:05.745 --> 00:53:08.940 align:middle line:84%
week before GDPR,
kayak.com, first customers.

00:53:08.940 --> 00:53:12.700 align:middle line:90%


00:53:12.700 --> 00:53:15.250 align:middle line:84%
Difference-in-difference is
it's a treatment control design.

00:53:15.250 --> 00:53:17.130 align:middle line:84%
They have five
treatment countries.

00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:19.530 align:middle line:84%
UK, France, Germany,
Italy, Spain.

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:21.780 align:middle line:84%
And then they have
three control countries.

00:53:21.780 --> 00:53:23.340 align:middle line:90%
US, Canada, Russia.

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:26.100 align:middle line:84%
And depending on which website
it is, some of the websites

00:53:26.100 --> 00:53:30.240 align:middle line:84%
are active in different
subsets of these countries,

00:53:30.240 --> 00:53:31.980 align:middle line:84%
but enough of them
have activities

00:53:31.980 --> 00:53:37.890 align:middle line:84%
both in treatment countries and
either Russia or North America

00:53:37.890 --> 00:53:40.770 align:middle line:84%
that they can then
do regressions

00:53:40.770 --> 00:53:43.890 align:middle line:84%
that have website fixed
effects and country fixed

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:46.110 align:middle line:84%
effects and time
fixed effects and try

00:53:46.110 --> 00:53:48.960 align:middle line:90%
to analyze the effects of GDPR.

00:53:48.960 --> 00:53:51.900 align:middle line:84%
They have some nice
simple pictures here,

00:53:51.900 --> 00:53:57.600 align:middle line:84%
showing you that GDPR
is having an effect.

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:00.900 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example
graphing the number of--

00:54:00.900 --> 00:54:06.180 align:middle line:84%
and the way GDPR works, if the
GDPR pop-up shows up and then

00:54:06.180 --> 00:54:11.790 align:middle line:84%
you click Don't Track Me,
this company never saw you

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:13.750 align:middle line:90%
because it's like Kayak--

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:17.340 align:middle line:90%
someone did the search on Kayak.

00:54:17.340 --> 00:54:20.950 align:middle line:84%
Kayak popped up the
track me or not box.

00:54:20.950 --> 00:54:22.660 align:middle line:90%
They click Do Not Track Me.

00:54:22.660 --> 00:54:25.620 align:middle line:84%
And then once they
click Do Not Track Me,

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:28.960 align:middle line:84%
they can't send the
query to whatever

00:54:28.960 --> 00:54:30.860 align:middle line:84%
this behind-the-scenes
intermediary is.

00:54:30.860 --> 00:54:34.540 align:middle line:84%
The behind [INAUDIBLE] never
get any message whatsoever.

00:54:34.540 --> 00:54:37.990 align:middle line:84%
And they specify that if the
behind-the-scenes intermediary

00:54:37.990 --> 00:54:40.710 align:middle line:84%
never gets a message, it's
basically this box isn't here,

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:43.210 align:middle line:84%
and just the lowest price flight
would have been in this box

00:54:43.210 --> 00:54:45.670 align:middle line:90%
instead.

00:54:45.670 --> 00:54:48.850 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so what they
track is they recognize

00:54:48.850 --> 00:54:54.940 align:middle line:84%
that GDPR is in effect by the
number of cookies that they--

00:54:54.940 --> 00:54:59.470 align:middle line:84%
the number of requests that
they get from Kayak drops.

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:03.070 align:middle line:84%
And what they show here
this is like the raw data

00:55:03.070 --> 00:55:05.295 align:middle line:84%
behind the
differences-in-differences.

00:55:05.295 --> 00:55:10.550 align:middle line:84%
This is the-- this
is the searches

00:55:10.550 --> 00:55:14.443 align:middle line:84%
that this intermediary's being
told about in the US and Canada,

00:55:14.443 --> 00:55:15.860 align:middle line:84%
and this is the
number of searches

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:18.500 align:middle line:84%
that they're being told about
in each of the EU countries.

00:55:18.500 --> 00:55:22.040 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that immediately
or perhaps even starting

00:55:22.040 --> 00:55:26.930 align:middle line:84%
the week before, there's
this big drop-off in searches

00:55:26.930 --> 00:55:31.650 align:middle line:84%
that they're being told about
in all the EU countries,

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:34.040 align:middle line:84%
and then there's not a
corresponding drop-off

00:55:34.040 --> 00:55:35.420 align:middle line:90%
in the US and Canada.

00:55:35.420 --> 00:55:37.860 align:middle line:84%
So that indicates that
GDPR is having an effect.

00:55:37.860 --> 00:55:41.450 align:middle line:84%
They also note in the data that
there are other firms who you

00:55:41.450 --> 00:55:43.760 align:middle line:84%
suspect-- and in this case,
they were able to verify--

00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:46.620 align:middle line:84%
did not actually comply
with GDPR right away.

00:55:46.620 --> 00:55:49.070 align:middle line:84%
And so in this
case, the searches

00:55:49.070 --> 00:55:52.100 align:middle line:84%
in one EU country and
the one non-EU country

00:55:52.100 --> 00:55:54.650 align:middle line:84%
that this website
served pretty much just

00:55:54.650 --> 00:55:56.810 align:middle line:84%
continued on the same
path across the line

00:55:56.810 --> 00:55:59.220 align:middle line:90%
and didn't have a big drop-off.

00:55:59.220 --> 00:56:01.040 align:middle line:84%
So somehow this
website, they were

00:56:01.040 --> 00:56:03.740 align:middle line:84%
able to then go back and
verify that website was not

00:56:03.740 --> 00:56:05.620 align:middle line:90%
GDPR-compliant right away.

00:56:05.620 --> 00:56:07.370 align:middle line:84%
And it took later to
come into compliance,

00:56:07.370 --> 00:56:10.530 align:middle line:90%
whereas this one did.

00:56:10.530 --> 00:56:14.760 align:middle line:84%
But when they do, it's
immediately apparent

00:56:14.760 --> 00:56:16.620 align:middle line:84%
that the regulations
are having an effect,

00:56:16.620 --> 00:56:20.310 align:middle line:84%
and they're seeing fewer
customers from those websites.

00:56:20.310 --> 00:56:23.460 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, it could be
that consumers go to Kayak

00:56:23.460 --> 00:56:25.162 align:middle line:84%
and do get annoyed
by the pop-up boxes

00:56:25.162 --> 00:56:26.620 align:middle line:84%
and never actually
do their search,

00:56:26.620 --> 00:56:30.795 align:middle line:84%
but this is-- fewer
people are doing a search

00:56:30.795 --> 00:56:33.420 align:middle line:84%
and then having the intermediary
being told about their search,

00:56:33.420 --> 00:56:34.920 align:middle line:84%
whether it's they're
never doing the search

00:56:34.920 --> 00:56:36.570 align:middle line:84%
or they do the search,
but they do the search

00:56:36.570 --> 00:56:37.820 align:middle line:90%
and they're not being tracked.

00:56:37.820 --> 00:56:40.970 align:middle line:90%


00:56:40.970 --> 00:56:43.130 align:middle line:90%
So what are the magnitudes?

00:56:43.130 --> 00:56:47.270 align:middle line:84%
First one is that while people
talk a lot about privacy,

00:56:47.270 --> 00:56:49.370 align:middle line:84%
it seems like most
consumers just

00:56:49.370 --> 00:56:51.920 align:middle line:84%
go with the-- just get
that thing off my screen

00:56:51.920 --> 00:56:55.010 align:middle line:84%
by just saying, yeah,
fine, I accept all cookies.

00:56:55.010 --> 00:56:57.350 align:middle line:84%
And I know I accept all
cookies all the time

00:56:57.350 --> 00:56:59.090 align:middle line:90%
on European websites.

00:56:59.090 --> 00:57:05.210 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is that
the log of unique cookies

00:57:05.210 --> 00:57:06.710 align:middle line:90%
goes down by 12%.

00:57:06.710 --> 00:57:10.550 align:middle line:84%
Log of recorded searches
goes down by 11%.

00:57:10.550 --> 00:57:15.380 align:middle line:84%
So there is this big drop-off
right at the same time that

00:57:15.380 --> 00:57:15.980 align:middle line:90%
people--

00:57:15.980 --> 00:57:17.755 align:middle line:84%
appears to be people
not giving consent.

00:57:17.755 --> 00:57:20.360 align:middle line:90%


00:57:20.360 --> 00:57:22.930 align:middle line:90%
Also, interesting here is the--

00:57:22.930 --> 00:57:25.670 align:middle line:90%


00:57:25.670 --> 00:57:28.790 align:middle line:84%
they sort of--
every consumer who

00:57:28.790 --> 00:57:31.760 align:middle line:84%
they are allowed to track,
every consumer they are allowed

00:57:31.760 --> 00:57:33.230 align:middle line:84%
to track, they
know how many times

00:57:33.230 --> 00:57:35.820 align:middle line:84%
they've seen that consumer
search in the past

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:37.820 align:middle line:84%
and how many searches are
recorded on the cookie

00:57:37.820 --> 00:57:41.000 align:middle line:84%
that they're putting
on that machine.

00:57:41.000 --> 00:57:44.712 align:middle line:84%
And so what they show
is a change in the--

00:57:44.712 --> 00:57:46.670 align:middle line:84%
so there's this distribution
of, how many times

00:57:46.670 --> 00:57:48.810 align:middle line:90%
have you seen a consumer?

00:57:48.810 --> 00:57:51.170 align:middle line:84%
And it's a histogram that's
like a lot of the consumers

00:57:51.170 --> 00:57:53.990 align:middle line:84%
you've seen exactly once,
and then some consumers

00:57:53.990 --> 00:57:56.660 align:middle line:84%
you've seen twice, and then many
consumers you've seen three,

00:57:56.660 --> 00:57:59.400 align:middle line:90%
four, or five times, whatever.

00:57:59.400 --> 00:58:01.760 align:middle line:84%
So you have this histogram
of how many times

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:05.190 align:middle line:84%
you have seen this
consumer before.

00:58:05.190 --> 00:58:08.250 align:middle line:84%
What they note is that
immediately after GDPR

00:58:08.250 --> 00:58:10.860 align:middle line:84%
goes into effect, the
number of consumers

00:58:10.860 --> 00:58:16.800 align:middle line:84%
that you've seen exactly one
time, this bar, goes way down.

00:58:16.800 --> 00:58:19.590 align:middle line:84%
And the number of consumers
you've seen two, three, four,

00:58:19.590 --> 00:58:21.090 align:middle line:84%
five, six, seven,
eight times seem

00:58:21.090 --> 00:58:25.640 align:middle line:84%
to just increase proportionally
and be very, very slightly.

00:58:25.640 --> 00:58:27.810 align:middle line:84%
So what they note
is that it seems

00:58:27.810 --> 00:58:31.320 align:middle line:84%
that when these
consumers now opt out,

00:58:31.320 --> 00:58:33.330 align:middle line:84%
you're seeing many fewer
consumers who you're

00:58:33.330 --> 00:58:35.520 align:middle line:84%
seeing exactly once, which
would be consumers who

00:58:35.520 --> 00:58:37.770 align:middle line:84%
may have something
already installed

00:58:37.770 --> 00:58:40.410 align:middle line:84%
to delete their
cookies or to block

00:58:40.410 --> 00:58:42.780 align:middle line:84%
the cookies from
identifying them

00:58:42.780 --> 00:58:45.422 align:middle line:84%
and make them look like a new
person every time they show up,

00:58:45.422 --> 00:58:47.880 align:middle line:84%
even though they're actually
someone who you're seeing over

00:58:47.880 --> 00:58:49.300 align:middle line:90%
and over and over again.

00:58:49.300 --> 00:58:50.910 align:middle line:84%
And so what they
note is that it seems

00:58:50.910 --> 00:58:54.030 align:middle line:84%
like this drop in the number
of people seen exactly once

00:58:54.030 --> 00:58:57.180 align:middle line:84%
makes you think that of
those 13% of the people

00:58:57.180 --> 00:58:59.820 align:middle line:84%
who dropped out or whatever
it is, many of them

00:58:59.820 --> 00:59:05.520 align:middle line:84%
also were cookie deleters or
had some blocking technology

00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:06.860 align:middle line:90%
they were already using.

00:59:06.860 --> 00:59:10.090 align:middle line:90%


00:59:10.090 --> 00:59:12.325 align:middle line:90%
So advertising revenue.

00:59:12.325 --> 00:59:14.680 align:middle line:84%
There's an immediate
drop in the number

00:59:14.680 --> 00:59:20.110 align:middle line:84%
of clicks that are recorded
on the compliant websites.

00:59:20.110 --> 00:59:22.870 align:middle line:90%
Number of clicks drop--

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:24.700 align:middle line:90%
I guess it's 13%.

00:59:24.700 --> 00:59:27.640 align:middle line:84%
Revenue goes down
16%, like, on the day

00:59:27.640 --> 00:59:29.330 align:middle line:90%
the thing takes into effect.

00:59:29.330 --> 00:59:31.080 align:middle line:90%
So this is certainly--

00:59:31.080 --> 00:59:33.667 align:middle line:84%
this is bad news for this
advertising intermediary

00:59:33.667 --> 00:59:35.000 align:middle line:90%
who we're getting the data from.

00:59:35.000 --> 00:59:36.500 align:middle line:90%
It's like they just--

00:59:36.500 --> 00:59:42.220 align:middle line:84%
13% fewer-- 13% fewer
clicks referred to them

00:59:42.220 --> 00:59:45.590 align:middle line:84%
to try to find an extra
potential to assess.

00:59:45.590 --> 00:59:47.710 align:middle line:84%
You know, you
presume they get paid

00:59:47.710 --> 00:59:50.530 align:middle line:84%
based on a per click basis
for how much revenue they

00:59:50.530 --> 00:59:52.030 align:middle line:90%
can produce for Kayak.

00:59:52.030 --> 00:59:54.190 align:middle line:84%
13% of the searches
disappear for them.

00:59:54.190 --> 00:59:56.590 align:middle line:90%
16% of the revenue disappears.

00:59:56.590 --> 00:59:59.170 align:middle line:84%
But then this interesting
effect they find

00:59:59.170 --> 01:00:06.680 align:middle line:84%
is that, you know, the bids
drop off or appear to be

01:00:06.680 --> 01:00:08.940 align:middle line:90%
slightly low on first week.

01:00:08.940 --> 01:00:10.880 align:middle line:84%
And then over the
next seven weeks,

01:00:10.880 --> 01:00:13.400 align:middle line:84%
there's just this
steady climb in what

01:00:13.400 --> 01:00:16.130 align:middle line:90%
people are bidding for the ads.

01:00:16.130 --> 01:00:17.810 align:middle line:84%
So again, the
bidding for the ads

01:00:17.810 --> 01:00:21.530 align:middle line:84%
is like bidding to what
American Airlines paid

01:00:21.530 --> 01:00:24.170 align:middle line:84%
to show that ad to me because
I'd done the Orange County

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:25.200 align:middle line:90%
search.

01:00:25.200 --> 01:00:27.530 align:middle line:84%
And what they show is that
there's nothing going on

01:00:27.530 --> 01:00:33.470 align:middle line:84%
in the first two weeks, but
then it's as if bids go up

01:00:33.470 --> 01:00:34.230 align:middle line:90%
and up and up.

01:00:34.230 --> 01:00:35.780 align:middle line:90%
So why would that happen?

01:00:35.780 --> 01:00:37.970 align:middle line:90%
Two potential explanations.

01:00:37.970 --> 01:00:40.945 align:middle line:84%
The people who are left
behind are just more valuable

01:00:40.945 --> 01:00:42.320 align:middle line:84%
because they're
different people.

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:43.905 align:middle line:84%
And those more
valuable people, they

01:00:43.905 --> 01:00:45.530 align:middle line:84%
didn't know that the
people left behind

01:00:45.530 --> 01:00:47.280 align:middle line:84%
would be more valuable
people, but they're

01:00:47.280 --> 01:00:50.070 align:middle line:84%
learning that those people left
behind are more valuable people,

01:00:50.070 --> 01:00:51.840 align:middle line:90%
and so the bids go up.

01:00:51.840 --> 01:00:57.080 align:middle line:84%
Or two, the intermediary is
better able to figure out

01:00:57.080 --> 01:00:59.100 align:middle line:90%
what those people want to buy.

01:00:59.100 --> 01:01:03.720 align:middle line:84%
So the intermediary may also
be providing American Airlines

01:01:03.720 --> 01:01:07.410 align:middle line:84%
and hotels.com predictions
of the probability

01:01:07.410 --> 01:01:09.390 align:middle line:84%
that I will buy
an American flight

01:01:09.390 --> 01:01:13.230 align:middle line:84%
or that I will rent a
hotel through hotels.com.

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:15.450 align:middle line:90%
The fact that they can tell--

01:01:15.450 --> 01:01:19.140 align:middle line:84%
provide better information
about me may get their bids

01:01:19.140 --> 01:01:20.830 align:middle line:90%
to go up in these auctions.

01:01:20.830 --> 01:01:23.640 align:middle line:84%
And so if the
intermediary is better

01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:26.370 align:middle line:84%
able to decide who is going to
purchase from Kayak versus who

01:01:26.370 --> 01:01:28.545 align:middle line:84%
would purchase
from another firm,

01:01:28.545 --> 01:01:30.420 align:middle line:84%
if they can do a better
job of data analysis,

01:01:30.420 --> 01:01:32.440 align:middle line:90%
that also makes prices go up.

01:01:32.440 --> 01:01:35.155 align:middle line:84%
So either people are better
or you're better figuring out

01:01:35.155 --> 01:01:36.780 align:middle line:84%
which of-- who are
the valuable people?

01:01:36.780 --> 01:01:41.885 align:middle line:84%
And that causes the average
prices to go up, OK?

01:01:41.885 --> 01:01:44.530 align:middle line:90%
And so this effect, it's not--

01:01:44.530 --> 01:01:47.470 align:middle line:90%


01:01:47.470 --> 01:01:51.305 align:middle line:84%
it's not like it's good news
for all the firms involved.

01:01:51.305 --> 01:01:52.430 align:middle line:90%
They can't track consumers.

01:01:52.430 --> 01:01:55.810 align:middle line:84%
But at least what's happened
here in the first seven weeks,

01:01:55.810 --> 01:01:57.790 align:middle line:84%
that's offsetting about
half of the decline

01:01:57.790 --> 01:01:59.030 align:middle line:90%
that you would have gotten.

01:01:59.030 --> 01:02:03.698 align:middle line:84%
So if you've gotten an initial
16% decline, it may be actually,

01:02:03.698 --> 01:02:05.740 align:middle line:84%
you're only going to get
an 8% decline in revenue

01:02:05.740 --> 01:02:09.035 align:middle line:84%
because the bids are going to go
up over time once people realize

01:02:09.035 --> 01:02:10.660 align:middle line:84%
who the consumers
are who are left over

01:02:10.660 --> 01:02:11.993 align:middle line:90%
and how well you can track them.

01:02:11.993 --> 01:02:14.960 align:middle line:90%


01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:15.460 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:02:15.460 --> 01:02:18.190 align:middle line:84%
And then the third thing
is they did have access--

01:02:18.190 --> 01:02:20.680 align:middle line:84%
this firm is doing a
machine learning algorithm

01:02:20.680 --> 01:02:23.110 align:middle line:84%
to predict whether you're going
to buy from kayak.com when

01:02:23.110 --> 01:02:25.225 align:middle line:90%
you search on kayak.com.

01:02:25.225 --> 01:02:29.830 align:middle line:84%
And so they have access
both to the predictions

01:02:29.830 --> 01:02:32.230 align:middle line:84%
that the intermediary made
at the time that the click--

01:02:32.230 --> 01:02:34.030 align:middle line:84%
at the time that
the search occurred

01:02:34.030 --> 01:02:38.200 align:middle line:84%
and whether the person actually
then bought from kayak.com

01:02:38.200 --> 01:02:40.090 align:middle line:90%
at the end of their session.

01:02:40.090 --> 01:02:46.405 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is that true
purchase frequencies increase.

01:02:46.405 --> 01:02:49.864 align:middle line:90%
And obviously, they can't--

01:02:49.864 --> 01:02:52.300 align:middle line:84%
they can't do-- this is not
a difference-in-difference

01:02:52.300 --> 01:02:54.490 align:middle line:84%
estimate because they don't
know whose purchases would have

01:02:54.490 --> 01:02:56.740 align:middle line:84%
been observed-- they're
observing this selected sample

01:02:56.740 --> 01:02:57.670 align:middle line:90%
afterwards.

01:02:57.670 --> 01:02:59.710 align:middle line:84%
But the selected
sample afterwards

01:02:59.710 --> 01:03:03.760 align:middle line:84%
is purchasing more often than
the unselected sample was

01:03:03.760 --> 01:03:05.050 align:middle line:90%
in the pre-period.

01:03:05.050 --> 01:03:09.300 align:middle line:84%
So these people are more
frequent purchasers.

01:03:09.300 --> 01:03:14.350 align:middle line:84%
And you know, when they look
at, how well is the intermediary

01:03:14.350 --> 01:03:17.030 align:middle line:84%
predicting purchases,
the mean squared error

01:03:17.030 --> 01:03:19.790 align:middle line:90%
goes up after the--

01:03:19.790 --> 01:03:21.480 align:middle line:84%
after the regulation
goes into effect,

01:03:21.480 --> 01:03:23.120 align:middle line:90%
so they're predicting worse.

01:03:23.120 --> 01:03:24.980 align:middle line:84%
But you know, mean
squared error is

01:03:24.980 --> 01:03:27.260 align:middle line:84%
sort of a problematic
measure of prediction

01:03:27.260 --> 01:03:31.490 align:middle line:84%
because the probability of
purchase is a very small number.

01:03:31.490 --> 01:03:34.620 align:middle line:90%
Like, it may be 10% or whatever.

01:03:34.620 --> 01:03:37.250 align:middle line:84%
And so if the mean
goes up, like if you're

01:03:37.250 --> 01:03:39.248 align:middle line:84%
trying to predict a
zero-one random variable,

01:03:39.248 --> 01:03:40.790 align:middle line:84%
zero-one random
variables are hardest

01:03:40.790 --> 01:03:42.390 align:middle line:84%
to predict when
their mean is 0.5.

01:03:42.390 --> 01:03:45.350 align:middle line:84%
If their mean is 0.001,
they're very easy to predict.

01:03:45.350 --> 01:03:48.380 align:middle line:84%
And so in mean squared error
sense, when the average purchase

01:03:48.380 --> 01:03:51.290 align:middle line:84%
frequency goes up, their
mean squared error goes up.

01:03:51.290 --> 01:03:53.040 align:middle line:84%
What they show is
that there's a graph--

01:03:53.040 --> 01:03:55.450 align:middle line:84%
I don't think I have
it here-- that the mean

01:03:55.450 --> 01:03:56.700 align:middle line:90%
squared error looks like this.

01:03:56.700 --> 01:03:58.460 align:middle line:84%
The mean squared error goes up,
and then the mean squared error

01:03:58.460 --> 01:03:59.520 align:middle line:90%
comes back down.

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:02.300 align:middle line:84%
So by the end of the sample,
even though the mean is higher,

01:04:02.300 --> 01:04:04.790 align:middle line:84%
the mean squared
error is not higher.

01:04:04.790 --> 01:04:08.630 align:middle line:84%
And so it seems that when the
firm is updating its machine

01:04:08.630 --> 01:04:12.530 align:middle line:84%
learning algorithms using
the new, better quality data,

01:04:12.530 --> 01:04:14.150 align:middle line:84%
it actually looks
like, if anything,

01:04:14.150 --> 01:04:16.260 align:middle line:84%
it's predicting better
what consumers are doing.

01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:18.422 align:middle line:84%
So it initially
suffers because it's

01:04:18.422 --> 01:04:19.880 align:middle line:84%
predicting in the
wrong environment

01:04:19.880 --> 01:04:22.070 align:middle line:84%
or for a selected
sample of people

01:04:22.070 --> 01:04:23.760 align:middle line:90%
using its unselected data.

01:04:23.760 --> 01:04:25.580 align:middle line:84%
But then once it does
it, it looks like--

01:04:25.580 --> 01:04:28.310 align:middle line:84%
the intermediary looks like it's
improving its predictive ability

01:04:28.310 --> 01:04:29.600 align:middle line:90%
in this new cleaner--

01:04:29.600 --> 01:04:32.450 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, the GDPR
has cleaned the data

01:04:32.450 --> 01:04:35.700 align:middle line:84%
for them a little bit and
made their job easier.

01:04:35.700 --> 01:04:36.200 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:04:36.200 --> 01:04:38.890 align:middle line:84%
At least it's not so
significantly easier,

01:04:38.890 --> 01:04:41.390 align:middle line:84%
but at least it's clear that
it didn't make their job harder

01:04:41.390 --> 01:04:42.390 align:middle line:90%
on the remaining people.

01:04:42.390 --> 01:04:47.650 align:middle line:90%


01:04:47.650 --> 01:04:48.150 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:48.150 --> 01:04:50.650 align:middle line:90%


01:04:50.650 --> 01:04:51.150 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:51.150 --> 01:04:54.550 align:middle line:84%
I think that's what I
had on Tobias's paper.

01:04:54.550 --> 01:04:56.945 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:04:56.945 --> 01:04:57.445 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:57.445 --> 01:05:00.820 align:middle line:90%
So I wanted to go to one other--

01:05:00.820 --> 01:05:02.710 align:middle line:90%
one other causal paper.

01:05:02.710 --> 01:05:06.010 align:middle line:90%
So this is an example of--

01:05:06.010 --> 01:05:12.280 align:middle line:84%
as I said, there's been a big
increase just in the last five

01:05:12.280 --> 01:05:17.335 align:middle line:84%
years or whatever of people
interested in just using

01:05:17.335 --> 01:05:20.200 align:middle line:84%
same kinds of
quasi-experimental methods

01:05:20.200 --> 01:05:22.660 align:middle line:84%
you see in many other
applied micro fields

01:05:22.660 --> 01:05:25.030 align:middle line:84%
to do advertising
studies and think about,

01:05:25.030 --> 01:05:27.280 align:middle line:84%
how do we estimate the
effect of advertising

01:05:27.280 --> 01:05:29.630 align:middle line:84%
using quasi-experimental
designs?

01:05:29.630 --> 01:05:32.320 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of
neat effects here.

01:05:32.320 --> 01:05:34.990 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of things
that do just sort of, again,

01:05:34.990 --> 01:05:36.910 align:middle line:84%
straight
difference-in-differences using

01:05:36.910 --> 01:05:39.730 align:middle line:84%
some kind of regulation goes
into effect banning advertising

01:05:39.730 --> 01:05:43.975 align:middle line:84%
or allowing
advertising, or someone

01:05:43.975 --> 01:05:46.390 align:middle line:84%
runs an advertising campaign
that lasts for four months.

01:05:46.390 --> 01:05:50.380 align:middle line:84%
And then it stops on one day,
and you use it before and after.

01:05:50.380 --> 01:05:52.480 align:middle line:84%
Spillovers from
political advertising

01:05:52.480 --> 01:05:55.450 align:middle line:84%
are a really neat
general purpose idea.

01:05:55.450 --> 01:05:58.150 align:middle line:84%
I guess-- maybe you
guys don't watch TV,

01:05:58.150 --> 01:06:00.795 align:middle line:84%
but if you're watching
TV in Massachusetts

01:06:00.795 --> 01:06:02.170 align:middle line:84%
right before an
election, you may

01:06:02.170 --> 01:06:05.230 align:middle line:84%
have noticed tons
and tons of campaigns

01:06:05.230 --> 01:06:08.350 align:middle line:84%
for Maggie Hassan versus
Don Bolduc in New Hampshire,

01:06:08.350 --> 01:06:11.260 align:middle line:84%
you know, buying up all the
ads on Massachusetts TV.

01:06:11.260 --> 01:06:15.820 align:middle line:84%
If you live in New Hampshire,
your entire TV for a month

01:06:15.820 --> 01:06:20.140 align:middle line:84%
before the election is
nothing but advertising--

01:06:20.140 --> 01:06:21.860 align:middle line:90%
political campaign ads.

01:06:21.860 --> 01:06:25.310 align:middle line:84%
The political campaign ads
shift other ads off the TV.

01:06:25.310 --> 01:06:27.970 align:middle line:84%
And you can do these
designs taking into effect

01:06:27.970 --> 01:06:30.190 align:middle line:84%
that New Hampshire
versus Vermont, no one

01:06:30.190 --> 01:06:33.923 align:middle line:84%
advertises on Vermont TV because
the democrats always win.

01:06:33.923 --> 01:06:35.590 align:middle line:84%
New Hampshire has a
ton of political ads

01:06:35.590 --> 01:06:37.130 align:middle line:90%
because it's a toss-up state.

01:06:37.130 --> 01:06:38.980 align:middle line:84%
You can do new
Hampshire versus Vermont

01:06:38.980 --> 01:06:42.460 align:middle line:84%
before and after an election and
see all these other advertisers

01:06:42.460 --> 01:06:44.350 align:middle line:84%
were getting blocked
out of New Hampshire,

01:06:44.350 --> 01:06:46.360 align:middle line:84%
but still advertising
in Vermont and Maine,

01:06:46.360 --> 01:06:50.230 align:middle line:84%
and use that as an
instrument for advertising.

01:06:50.230 --> 01:06:52.400 align:middle line:84%
There are papers
using sporting events.

01:06:52.400 --> 01:06:54.580 align:middle line:84%
If your team gets
to the Super Bowl,

01:06:54.580 --> 01:06:57.210 align:middle line:84%
you therefore see all--
everyone watches the Super Bowl.

01:06:57.210 --> 01:07:00.150 align:middle line:84%
They see many more Budweiser ads
than they would see otherwise.

01:07:00.150 --> 01:07:02.000 align:middle line:90%
How does that affect purchasing?

01:07:02.000 --> 01:07:08.570 align:middle line:84%
In some ways, I talked about
the Bronnenberg, Dubé, Gentzkow

01:07:08.570 --> 01:07:11.970 align:middle line:84%
paper about people moving
from one city to another.

01:07:11.970 --> 01:07:13.640 align:middle line:84%
If you move from
one city to another,

01:07:13.640 --> 01:07:15.860 align:middle line:84%
you're seeing different
ads when you've moved there

01:07:15.860 --> 01:07:17.660 align:middle line:84%
that could be an exogenous
difference in the ads

01:07:17.660 --> 01:07:18.450 align:middle line:90%
that you've seen.

01:07:18.450 --> 01:07:22.370 align:middle line:90%
We can use ideas like that.

01:07:22.370 --> 01:07:27.500 align:middle line:84%
Brad Shapiro's paper is
using what we call a border

01:07:27.500 --> 01:07:30.290 align:middle line:90%
design in media markets.

01:07:30.290 --> 01:07:35.580 align:middle line:84%
So the application here is
to antidepressant drugs.

01:07:35.580 --> 01:07:38.870 align:middle line:84%
So there was a class of
antidepressant drugs called

01:07:38.870 --> 01:07:39.680 align:middle line:90%
SSRIs.

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:41.300 align:middle line:90%
I forget what that stands for.

01:07:41.300 --> 01:07:44.090 align:middle line:84%
Introduced in the
late '80s and '90s.

01:07:44.090 --> 01:07:47.667 align:middle line:84%
These drugs were under patent
protection and earned billions

01:07:47.667 --> 01:07:50.000 align:middle line:84%
and billions of dollars a
year in revenues in the period

01:07:50.000 --> 01:07:54.350 align:middle line:84%
he's studying, which is
late '90s to early 2000s.

01:07:54.350 --> 01:07:57.520 align:middle line:90%
And you know, these are--

01:07:57.520 --> 01:08:00.580 align:middle line:84%
this is just a picture of--
that's what a Zoloft ad on TV

01:08:00.580 --> 01:08:01.600 align:middle line:90%
would look like.

01:08:01.600 --> 01:08:03.070 align:middle line:90%
You'd see this ad.

01:08:03.070 --> 01:08:06.200 align:middle line:84%
This is the guy who's depressed
and he's under a cloud,

01:08:06.200 --> 01:08:08.960 align:middle line:84%
and symptoms persist every
day for at least two weeks.

01:08:08.960 --> 01:08:11.020 align:middle line:84%
And then later in
the commercial,

01:08:11.020 --> 01:08:12.070 align:middle line:90%
somehow he takes Zoloft.

01:08:12.070 --> 01:08:14.980 align:middle line:84%
And the clouds part, and the
sun comes out, and he's happy.

01:08:14.980 --> 01:08:17.770 align:middle line:84%
And then you have this
woman talking about

01:08:17.770 --> 01:08:21.260 align:middle line:90%
how, I couldn't do anything.

01:08:21.260 --> 01:08:23.300 align:middle line:84%
My life now-- my life
is so much better.

01:08:23.300 --> 01:08:27.310 align:middle line:84%
And then obviously, then that
10-second comment from her,

01:08:27.310 --> 01:08:31.600 align:middle line:84%
followed by this long litany
of, may cause suicidal thoughts,

01:08:31.600 --> 01:08:34.090 align:middle line:84%
may cause
gastrointestinal symptoms,

01:08:34.090 --> 01:08:38.260 align:middle line:84%
may cause loss of appetite, may
cause cancer of various kinds.

01:08:38.260 --> 01:08:40.584 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, it was sort of--

01:08:40.584 --> 01:08:42.368 align:middle line:90%
[SIGH]

01:08:42.368 --> 01:08:44.740 align:middle line:90%
It was-- I don't know.

01:08:44.740 --> 01:08:48.939 align:middle line:84%
Again, prior to 1997, TV
commercials were not--

01:08:48.939 --> 01:08:50.677 align:middle line:84%
were not-- there were
not TV commercials

01:08:50.677 --> 01:08:52.510 align:middle line:84%
for prescription drugs
in the United States.

01:08:52.510 --> 01:08:55.540 align:middle line:84%
Prior to 1997,
there was no TV ad--

01:08:55.540 --> 01:08:58.040 align:middle line:84%
the United States was like every
other country in the world.

01:08:58.040 --> 01:09:02.134 align:middle line:84%
There was no
advertising for drugs.

01:09:02.134 --> 01:09:04.580 align:middle line:84%
Rules were changed for
how many of those warnings

01:09:04.580 --> 01:09:07.260 align:middle line:84%
you had to put in
an ad around 1997.

01:09:07.260 --> 01:09:10.729 align:middle line:84%
And so 1999, it became
feasible to have

01:09:10.729 --> 01:09:13.399 align:middle line:84%
only 20 seconds of your ad
with all the side effects

01:09:13.399 --> 01:09:16.460 align:middle line:84%
and to still have an ad that
survived with all the side

01:09:16.460 --> 01:09:17.600 align:middle line:90%
effect stuff.

01:09:17.600 --> 01:09:21.689 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, first depression
ads appeared in 1999.

01:09:21.689 --> 01:09:24.694 align:middle line:84%
What does Brad
have in this paper?

01:09:24.694 --> 01:09:26.300 align:middle line:90%
He's got prescription data.

01:09:26.300 --> 01:09:30.170 align:middle line:84%
So the prescription data is a
5% random sample of physicians

01:09:30.170 --> 01:09:32.850 align:middle line:90%
who prescribe antidepressants.

01:09:32.850 --> 01:09:35.370 align:middle line:84%
The data set also includes
physician characteristics,

01:09:35.370 --> 01:09:37.380 align:middle line:84%
including the exact
office address.

01:09:37.380 --> 01:09:39.450 align:middle line:84%
Using the exact office
address, he can then

01:09:39.450 --> 01:09:41.439 align:middle line:90%
map each physician to a county.

01:09:41.439 --> 01:09:44.354 align:middle line:84%
Now obviously, not
all patients visit

01:09:44.354 --> 01:09:46.229 align:middle line:84%
a physician in the county
in which they live,

01:09:46.229 --> 01:09:48.460 align:middle line:84%
but these are relatively
rural counties,

01:09:48.460 --> 01:09:50.560 align:middle line:84%
so we hope that's not
such a bad assumption.

01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:53.550 align:middle line:84%
And then he takes the
physician-level data,

01:09:53.550 --> 01:09:55.628 align:middle line:84%
aggregates that to the
county month level.

01:09:55.628 --> 01:09:58.170 align:middle line:84%
And most the analysis is going
to be done at the county month

01:09:58.170 --> 01:09:59.268 align:middle line:90%
level.

01:09:59.268 --> 01:10:01.560 align:middle line:84%
And I guess, continuing the
theme on many of the papers

01:10:01.560 --> 01:10:04.560 align:middle line:84%
we look at, it is using
an impressive data set.

01:10:04.560 --> 01:10:08.170 align:middle line:84%
And you need detailed data sets
to write good papers these days.

01:10:08.170 --> 01:10:10.720 align:middle line:84%
So what does he
have on advertising?

01:10:10.720 --> 01:10:12.900 align:middle line:84%
He has TV advertising
expenditures

01:10:12.900 --> 01:10:19.320 align:middle line:84%
by brand in each of 101 markets
in the US from September 1999

01:10:19.320 --> 01:10:21.210 align:middle line:90%
to December 2003.

01:10:21.210 --> 01:10:23.640 align:middle line:84%
So the basic
advertising data set

01:10:23.640 --> 01:10:27.690 align:middle line:84%
is at the DMA, which is like--
you can think of as roughly

01:10:27.690 --> 01:10:32.210 align:middle line:84%
as a city, the DMA
times month level.

01:10:32.210 --> 01:10:36.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, much of the advertising
of pharmaceuticals is national,

01:10:36.630 --> 01:10:39.550 align:middle line:84%
but there is substantial
local advertising also.

01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:42.570 align:middle line:84%
So what he does is he creates
a measure of local advertising

01:10:42.570 --> 01:10:45.270 align:middle line:84%
in each market by
basically apportioning

01:10:45.270 --> 01:10:48.150 align:middle line:84%
the national spending
according to each DMA's

01:10:48.150 --> 01:10:50.160 align:middle line:84%
share of the
national population,

01:10:50.160 --> 01:10:52.860 align:middle line:84%
and then adding on top of
that the advertising that's

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:57.700 align:middle line:90%
purely local to that DMA.

01:10:57.700 --> 01:10:59.470 align:middle line:84%
Shapiro's strategy
for identification

01:10:59.470 --> 01:11:02.380 align:middle line:84%
is to limit the
analysis to DMA borders

01:11:02.380 --> 01:11:05.320 align:middle line:84%
and to treat monthly advertising
as exogenous once you include

01:11:05.320 --> 01:11:07.960 align:middle line:84%
the product-border-quarter
and product-border-DMA fixed

01:11:07.960 --> 01:11:08.713 align:middle line:90%
effects.

01:11:08.713 --> 01:11:09.880 align:middle line:90%
So why does that make sense?

01:11:09.880 --> 01:11:12.640 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that you didn't
have just the borders here,

01:11:12.640 --> 01:11:15.940 align:middle line:84%
and you were going to try
to compare advertising

01:11:15.940 --> 01:11:18.970 align:middle line:84%
and sales in the Columbus area
with advertising and sales

01:11:18.970 --> 01:11:20.410 align:middle line:90%
in the Cleveland area.

01:11:20.410 --> 01:11:23.980 align:middle line:84%
You know, there, you'd really
worry that inherent demand is

01:11:23.980 --> 01:11:26.140 align:middle line:84%
different in Columbus
than it is from Cleveland

01:11:26.140 --> 01:11:27.760 align:middle line:84%
and that the firms
who are advertising

01:11:27.760 --> 01:11:30.177 align:middle line:84%
know that demand is different
from Columbus and Cleveland.

01:11:30.177 --> 01:11:32.485 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
advertising in Columbus

01:11:32.485 --> 01:11:35.530 align:middle line:84%
is correlated with the
demand in Columbus.

01:11:35.530 --> 01:11:37.450 align:middle line:84%
The idea of the border
effect is to say

01:11:37.450 --> 01:11:39.430 align:middle line:84%
that it's kind of like
the same thing you would

01:11:39.430 --> 01:11:42.800 align:middle line:84%
do in a regression discontinuity
design, but in a spatial sense.

01:11:42.800 --> 01:11:50.920 align:middle line:84%
So you know, if you look at
Columbus versus Cleveland DMA,

01:11:50.920 --> 01:11:56.320 align:middle line:84%
you see one, two, three, four,
five counties along the Columbus

01:11:56.320 --> 01:11:57.540 align:middle line:90%
side of the border.

01:11:57.540 --> 01:12:01.760 align:middle line:84%
And then you see one,
two, three, four counties

01:12:01.760 --> 01:12:04.250 align:middle line:84%
along the Cleveland
side of the border.

01:12:04.250 --> 01:12:05.690 align:middle line:84%
You know, these
counties are going

01:12:05.690 --> 01:12:07.700 align:middle line:90%
to be relatively rural counties.

01:12:07.700 --> 01:12:09.980 align:middle line:84%
They're going to be fairly
far from the center--

01:12:09.980 --> 01:12:13.010 align:middle line:84%
the cities that are the
center of these DMAs.

01:12:13.010 --> 01:12:16.580 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is supposed to be
that the people on the Columbus

01:12:16.580 --> 01:12:20.840 align:middle line:84%
side, the people at the
Cleveland side are very similar.

01:12:20.840 --> 01:12:25.970 align:middle line:84%
The advertising that
the Columbus people see

01:12:25.970 --> 01:12:30.110 align:middle line:84%
is not going to be driven by the
characteristics of the people

01:12:30.110 --> 01:12:31.970 align:middle line:84%
on the Columbus
side of the border

01:12:31.970 --> 01:12:34.220 align:middle line:84%
because the people on the
Columbus side of the border

01:12:34.220 --> 01:12:38.090 align:middle line:84%
buy perhaps, you know, one
or 5% of the prescriptions

01:12:38.090 --> 01:12:41.278 align:middle line:84%
of antidepressants in the
Columbus DMA prescriptions

01:12:41.278 --> 01:12:43.070 align:middle line:84%
are going to be-- you
know, the advertising

01:12:43.070 --> 01:12:45.612 align:middle line:84%
will be driven by something--
a factor affecting the Columbus

01:12:45.612 --> 01:12:47.540 align:middle line:90%
DMA or the Cleveland DMA.

01:12:47.540 --> 01:12:51.710 align:middle line:84%
And so you can treat the
advertising choices as exogenous

01:12:51.710 --> 01:12:54.650 align:middle line:84%
once you put in these
product-border-quarter

01:12:54.650 --> 01:12:57.180 align:middle line:84%
and product-border
by DMA fixed effects.

01:12:57.180 --> 01:12:59.678 align:middle line:84%
And again, the
product-border-DMA fixed

01:12:59.678 --> 01:13:01.220 align:middle line:84%
effects, those are
going to capture--

01:13:01.220 --> 01:13:03.220 align:middle line:84%
if the people in the
Columbus side of the border

01:13:03.220 --> 01:13:05.553 align:middle line:84%
are slightly more depressed
than people on the Cleveland

01:13:05.553 --> 01:13:06.980 align:middle line:84%
side of the border,
that would get

01:13:06.980 --> 01:13:10.730 align:middle line:84%
captured by that nontime-varying
fixed effect for the Columbus

01:13:10.730 --> 01:13:13.160 align:middle line:90%
side of the border, OK?

01:13:13.160 --> 01:13:15.710 align:middle line:84%
So the basic exercise
here is to just

01:13:15.710 --> 01:13:17.780 align:middle line:84%
put in all those fixed
effects and regress

01:13:17.780 --> 01:13:20.660 align:middle line:90%
the log of sales in each--

01:13:20.660 --> 01:13:24.350 align:middle line:84%
on each side of the border
in a month on the advertising

01:13:24.350 --> 01:13:27.660 align:middle line:84%
on each side of the border in a
month, plus these fixed effects.

01:13:27.660 --> 01:13:28.970 align:middle line:90%
And what do you find?

01:13:28.970 --> 01:13:31.490 align:middle line:84%
So first thing that he
finds is that advertising

01:13:31.490 --> 01:13:33.230 align:middle line:90%
has large effects.

01:13:33.230 --> 01:13:36.470 align:middle line:84%
So look at the first number
that I put in the box there.

01:13:36.470 --> 01:13:38.480 align:middle line:90%
The DTC has a--

01:13:38.480 --> 01:13:40.520 align:middle line:84%
direct to consumer
advertising in this market

01:13:40.520 --> 01:13:42.800 align:middle line:90%
has a coefficient of 0.024.

01:13:42.800 --> 01:13:44.990 align:middle line:84%
I believe the right-hand
side variable's

01:13:44.990 --> 01:13:47.820 align:middle line:84%
in the scale of
pennies per person.

01:13:47.820 --> 01:13:50.930 align:middle line:84%
So this is if you conduct an
advertising campaign that costs

01:13:50.930 --> 01:13:57.320 align:middle line:84%
$0.01 per person in that area,
in the DMA you would get direct

01:13:57.320 --> 01:13:58.670 align:middle line:90%
to consumer-- you would get--

01:13:58.670 --> 01:14:01.620 align:middle line:90%
sales go up by 2.4%.

01:14:01.620 --> 01:14:02.120 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:14:02.120 --> 01:14:04.070 align:middle line:84%
Second significant
effect you see here

01:14:04.070 --> 01:14:06.300 align:middle line:84%
is the spillover of
rival advertising.

01:14:06.300 --> 01:14:09.350 align:middle line:84%
So spillover rival advertising
is a coefficient of 0.016,

01:14:09.350 --> 01:14:14.490 align:middle line:84%
which is roughly 2/3 of the size
of the own advertising effect.

01:14:14.490 --> 01:14:16.880 align:middle line:84%
So that is, you know, it's
better to advertise yourself,

01:14:16.880 --> 01:14:19.910 align:middle line:84%
but having a rival advertise
gives you almost 2/3

01:14:19.910 --> 01:14:21.140 align:middle line:90%
of the sales boost.

01:14:21.140 --> 01:14:24.860 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, you don't have to
pay for the rival advertising.

01:14:24.860 --> 01:14:29.210 align:middle line:84%
Second result here, if you look
at the quadratic terms, this one

01:14:29.210 --> 01:14:33.350 align:middle line:84%
and this one, quadratic terms
have negative coefficients

01:14:33.350 --> 01:14:36.320 align:middle line:84%
on them, which means that
there is decreasing returns

01:14:36.320 --> 01:14:37.880 align:middle line:90%
to advertising.

01:14:37.880 --> 01:14:41.900 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the shape
of those quadratic things,

01:14:41.900 --> 01:14:46.370 align:middle line:84%
functions, it would be
that advertising maxes out

01:14:46.370 --> 01:14:49.520 align:middle line:84%
at about five and a half on
the own advertising, nine

01:14:49.520 --> 01:14:51.090 align:middle line:90%
on the rival advertising.

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:53.780 align:middle line:84%
Those levels are well above the
levels of actual advertising

01:14:53.780 --> 01:14:56.090 align:middle line:84%
the data, so it's just
capturing the curvature

01:14:56.090 --> 01:14:59.750 align:middle line:84%
that there are decreasing
returns as you advertise more.

01:14:59.750 --> 01:15:03.790 align:middle line:84%
And then the third important
effect in this regression

01:15:03.790 --> 01:15:07.250 align:middle line:84%
is the DTC times DTT
rival coefficient.

01:15:07.250 --> 01:15:10.240 align:middle line:84%
What that says is that
the marginal return

01:15:10.240 --> 01:15:15.548 align:middle line:84%
to my advertising goes down when
my rival is advertising more.

01:15:15.548 --> 01:15:17.090 align:middle line:84%
You know, that fits
with the general.

01:15:17.090 --> 01:15:19.900 align:middle line:84%
If there's decreasing returns
to aggregate advertising,

01:15:19.900 --> 01:15:20.800 align:middle line:90%
that would be true.

01:15:20.800 --> 01:15:23.020 align:middle line:84%
As my rival
advertises more, we're

01:15:23.020 --> 01:15:25.750 align:middle line:84%
already getting further out
on the decreasing return side

01:15:25.750 --> 01:15:28.810 align:middle line:84%
and that's going to
cause a reduced benefit

01:15:28.810 --> 01:15:30.020 align:middle line:90%
from advertising.

01:15:30.020 --> 01:15:32.600 align:middle line:90%


01:15:32.600 --> 01:15:36.680 align:middle line:84%
And it's fairly simple
estimation strategy,

01:15:36.680 --> 01:15:40.190 align:middle line:84%
but I think-- you know, fairly
simple estimation strategy,

01:15:40.190 --> 01:15:42.532 align:middle line:84%
you just have to buy these
regression discontinuity-type

01:15:42.532 --> 01:15:44.240 align:middle line:84%
assumptions that the
people along the two

01:15:44.240 --> 01:15:47.000 align:middle line:84%
sides of the border are similar
other than a fixed effect that's

01:15:47.000 --> 01:15:50.060 align:middle line:84%
not time varying and that
the advertising is not

01:15:50.060 --> 01:15:52.895 align:middle line:84%
being driven by something
that's border-specific.

01:15:52.895 --> 01:15:54.770 align:middle line:84%
It would be something
that if it's anything--

01:15:54.770 --> 01:15:56.330 align:middle line:84%
if it's not just
purely random, it's

01:15:56.330 --> 01:15:58.865 align:middle line:84%
driven by something
related to the cities,

01:15:58.865 --> 01:16:00.740 align:middle line:84%
and therefore wouldn't
have different effects

01:16:00.740 --> 01:16:02.282 align:middle line:84%
on the different
sides of the border.

01:16:02.282 --> 01:16:04.880 align:middle line:90%


01:16:04.880 --> 01:16:07.700 align:middle line:84%
The next thing Shapiro does,
though, this is-- you know,

01:16:07.700 --> 01:16:09.800 align:middle line:84%
like many papers,
it both combines

01:16:09.800 --> 01:16:13.850 align:middle line:84%
a standard, modern
causal inference approach

01:16:13.850 --> 01:16:15.970 align:middle line:90%
with a structural estimation.

01:16:15.970 --> 01:16:18.770 align:middle line:84%
So his structural
estimation is basically

01:16:18.770 --> 01:16:21.320 align:middle line:90%
driven off the same levels of--

01:16:21.320 --> 01:16:23.930 align:middle line:84%
the same exogenous variation
that's driving the reduced form

01:16:23.930 --> 01:16:24.930 align:middle line:90%
estimates.

01:16:24.930 --> 01:16:28.860 align:middle line:84%
But here, he does this
nested logit model of demand.

01:16:28.860 --> 01:16:31.520 align:middle line:84%
The nested logit model
has three levels.

01:16:31.520 --> 01:16:34.440 align:middle line:84%
At the top level, there's a
choice between an inside good

01:16:34.440 --> 01:16:35.890 align:middle line:90%
and the outside good.

01:16:35.890 --> 01:16:40.380 align:middle line:84%
That choice is driven in part by
total advertising in the market.

01:16:40.380 --> 01:16:42.770 align:middle line:84%
The second level is a
choice between categories.

01:16:42.770 --> 01:16:45.660 align:middle line:84%
I think I mentioned there
are six different categories

01:16:45.660 --> 01:16:48.940 align:middle line:84%
of antidepressant drugs
being sold at the time.

01:16:48.940 --> 01:16:50.640 align:middle line:90%
One of them is "other."

01:16:50.640 --> 01:16:55.110 align:middle line:84%
And so that choice is driven by
his right-hand side variables,

01:16:55.110 --> 01:16:58.470 align:middle line:84%
which is advertising within
that particular category

01:16:58.470 --> 01:16:59.650 align:middle line:90%
of antidepressant.

01:16:59.650 --> 01:17:01.380 align:middle line:84%
And then the bottom
level, which is

01:17:01.380 --> 01:17:04.320 align:middle line:84%
going to be the most purely
business dealing level,

01:17:04.320 --> 01:17:07.770 align:middle line:90%
is competition between--

01:17:07.770 --> 01:17:11.130 align:middle line:84%
people make a choice of
which drug within a class

01:17:11.130 --> 01:17:13.463 align:middle line:90%
of antidepressants they buy.

01:17:13.463 --> 01:17:15.880 align:middle line:84%
Again, that's going to have
advertising on the right side.

01:17:15.880 --> 01:17:18.720 align:middle line:84%
That's advertising of
the particular molecule.

01:17:18.720 --> 01:17:21.540 align:middle line:84%
At least early in his data,
there are actually only two

01:17:21.540 --> 01:17:23.860 align:middle line:90%
of the firms doing advertising.

01:17:23.860 --> 01:17:26.820 align:middle line:84%
There's GlaxoSmithKline
advertising Paxil

01:17:26.820 --> 01:17:31.320 align:middle line:84%
and there's Eli Lilly
advertising Prozac, OK?

01:17:31.320 --> 01:17:33.242 align:middle line:84%
What do the advertising
variables look like?

01:17:33.242 --> 01:17:34.950 align:middle line:84%
You know, the advertising
variable that's

01:17:34.950 --> 01:17:36.390 align:middle line:90%
on the right side of this--

01:17:36.390 --> 01:17:44.250 align:middle line:84%
so advertising for Product
J, Order Level B, Time T

01:17:44.250 --> 01:17:49.440 align:middle line:84%
is going to be a sum
of delta to the s times

01:17:49.440 --> 01:17:59.100 align:middle line:84%
advertising by that firm in the
same region at time t minus s.

01:17:59.100 --> 01:18:02.700 align:middle line:84%
So he estimates these discount
factors, delta to the s,

01:18:02.700 --> 01:18:06.870 align:middle line:84%
to estimate, how quickly
does advertising die out?

01:18:06.870 --> 01:18:09.550 align:middle line:84%
Again, there are three separate
deltas being estimated.

01:18:09.550 --> 01:18:13.740 align:middle line:84%
There's a delta being estimated
for the aggregate demand

01:18:13.740 --> 01:18:15.580 align:middle line:84%
for the inside good
versus the outside good.

01:18:15.580 --> 01:18:19.090 align:middle line:84%
There's a delta being estimated
for the within category effect.

01:18:19.090 --> 01:18:22.980 align:middle line:84%
There's a delta being estimated
for the product effect.

01:18:22.980 --> 01:18:24.930 align:middle line:84%
Biggest results he has
there on those deltas

01:18:24.930 --> 01:18:27.420 align:middle line:84%
is that the deltas
are fairly large,

01:18:27.420 --> 01:18:30.160 align:middle line:84%
but the delta is-- or
sorry, fairly small.

01:18:30.160 --> 01:18:32.760 align:middle line:84%
Advertising dries
out fairly quickly,

01:18:32.760 --> 01:18:35.400 align:middle line:84%
and it dries out at
different-- it dies out

01:18:35.400 --> 01:18:37.840 align:middle line:84%
at different speeds at
the different levels.

01:18:37.840 --> 01:18:44.860 align:middle line:84%
So it dies out most quickly
at the within category level.

01:18:44.860 --> 01:18:47.370 align:middle line:84%
There, he finds that
within three months,

01:18:47.370 --> 01:18:49.140 align:middle line:84%
90% of the benefit
of advertising

01:18:49.140 --> 01:18:51.810 align:middle line:84%
has died out in terms of getting
share to you versus other firms

01:18:51.810 --> 01:18:53.320 align:middle line:90%
selling similar drugs.

01:18:53.320 --> 01:18:57.250 align:middle line:84%
And then it dries out more
slowly but still fairly quickly

01:18:57.250 --> 01:18:58.440 align:middle line:90%
at the aggregate level.

01:18:58.440 --> 01:19:02.290 align:middle line:84%
There, it takes six months for
90% of the demand to die out.

01:19:02.290 --> 01:19:04.740 align:middle line:84%
So in the graph I put
on here at the right,

01:19:04.740 --> 01:19:05.770 align:middle line:90%
I graphed these effects.

01:19:05.770 --> 01:19:08.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is looking at what
the marginal effect would

01:19:08.350 --> 01:19:10.810 align:middle line:84%
be if Zoloft started
an advertising

01:19:10.810 --> 01:19:15.700 align:middle line:84%
campaign of some size at
the beginning of 2002.

01:19:15.700 --> 01:19:17.920 align:middle line:84%
The red line at the
bottom of that graph

01:19:17.920 --> 01:19:21.910 align:middle line:84%
shows how many extra
prescriptions per month Zoloft

01:19:21.910 --> 01:19:23.930 align:middle line:90%
gets in thousands.

01:19:23.930 --> 01:19:28.990 align:middle line:84%
And so what they're getting
is 20,000 extra prescriptions

01:19:28.990 --> 01:19:30.470 align:middle line:90%
in the first month.

01:19:30.470 --> 01:19:33.710 align:middle line:84%
But then as I said,
much of that dies out.

01:19:33.710 --> 01:19:38.498 align:middle line:84%
And so you can see by
three months later,

01:19:38.498 --> 01:19:40.040 align:middle line:84%
many of those
prescriptions are lost,

01:19:40.040 --> 01:19:42.920 align:middle line:84%
and then the effect
continues to die out.

01:19:42.920 --> 01:19:46.050 align:middle line:84%
The black line-- or
is it dark blue?--

01:19:46.050 --> 01:19:49.560 align:middle line:84%
in contrast, is this is the
gains in total prescriptions.

01:19:49.560 --> 01:19:52.580 align:middle line:84%
So while Zoloft prescriptions
go up by 20,000,

01:19:52.580 --> 01:19:54.750 align:middle line:84%
total prescriptions
go up by 70,000.

01:19:54.750 --> 01:19:58.700 align:middle line:84%
So that's saying 50,000 extra
prescriptions are being gained

01:19:58.700 --> 01:20:03.605 align:middle line:84%
by other firms, not the
manufacturer of Zoloft.

01:20:03.605 --> 01:20:05.070 align:middle line:90%
Those also die out.

01:20:05.070 --> 01:20:08.270 align:middle line:84%
And here, we see those
dying out more slowly.

01:20:08.270 --> 01:20:11.400 align:middle line:84%
So at the beginning,
it's 20,000 and 50,000.

01:20:11.400 --> 01:20:13.400 align:middle line:84%
The other firms are getting
two and a half times

01:20:13.400 --> 01:20:14.807 align:middle line:90%
what Zoloft is getting.

01:20:14.807 --> 01:20:17.390 align:middle line:84%
The dashed line here is showing
that by the end of the sample,

01:20:17.390 --> 01:20:19.432 align:middle line:84%
the other firms are getting
five and a half times

01:20:19.432 --> 01:20:21.410 align:middle line:84%
what Zoloft manufacturers
are getting.

01:20:21.410 --> 01:20:24.620 align:middle line:84%
So this is showing that there
is really substantial spillovers

01:20:24.620 --> 01:20:25.570 align:middle line:90%
to the rival firms.

01:20:25.570 --> 01:20:28.430 align:middle line:90%


01:20:28.430 --> 01:20:30.920 align:middle line:84%
Next topic he does in
the structural model

01:20:30.920 --> 01:20:33.933 align:middle line:84%
is asking how much
advertising the firms do

01:20:33.933 --> 01:20:36.350 align:middle line:84%
relative to what the model
says would be the optimal level

01:20:36.350 --> 01:20:37.570 align:middle line:90%
of advertising.

01:20:37.570 --> 01:20:39.980 align:middle line:84%
You know, many, many
structural papers

01:20:39.980 --> 01:20:43.130 align:middle line:84%
impose the supply side
first order conditions,

01:20:43.130 --> 01:20:46.790 align:middle line:84%
imposing that firms
are behaving optimally.

01:20:46.790 --> 01:20:48.410 align:middle line:84%
Shapiro does not
want to do that.

01:20:48.410 --> 01:20:51.800 align:middle line:84%
His argument for why he does not
want to do that is advertising

01:20:51.800 --> 01:20:52.670 align:middle line:90%
is brand new.

01:20:52.670 --> 01:20:55.970 align:middle line:84%
It's being done for the first
time in the United States.

01:20:55.970 --> 01:20:57.918 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't really happen
elsewhere in the world.

01:20:57.918 --> 01:20:59.960 align:middle line:84%
Why would the firms know
what the marginal return

01:20:59.960 --> 01:21:02.210 align:middle line:84%
to advertising is until
they've been doing advertising

01:21:02.210 --> 01:21:04.460 align:middle line:90%
for some period of time?

01:21:04.460 --> 01:21:06.770 align:middle line:84%
What he finds in
his simulation is

01:21:06.770 --> 01:21:09.320 align:middle line:84%
that it looks like the
advertising-- given

01:21:09.320 --> 01:21:11.330 align:middle line:84%
the advertising is
effective, firms

01:21:11.330 --> 01:21:14.420 align:middle line:84%
should be doing much more
advertising than they do.

01:21:14.420 --> 01:21:18.020 align:middle line:84%
In particular, in the early part
of the sample when it is just

01:21:18.020 --> 01:21:20.480 align:middle line:84%
Paxil and Prozac
being advertised,

01:21:20.480 --> 01:21:23.870 align:middle line:84%
the firm should be
advertising four times more

01:21:23.870 --> 01:21:25.550 align:middle line:84%
than they do if they
did the equilibrium

01:21:25.550 --> 01:21:31.130 align:middle line:84%
level of advertising, even
given the free rider problem.

01:21:31.130 --> 01:21:32.910 align:middle line:90%
It's different across the firms.

01:21:32.910 --> 01:21:35.870 align:middle line:84%
You know, Paxil, which doesn't
have a big market share,

01:21:35.870 --> 01:21:37.700 align:middle line:84%
or doesn't have a
very big market share,

01:21:37.700 --> 01:21:40.530 align:middle line:84%
is doing about as much
advertising as it should.

01:21:40.530 --> 01:21:44.600 align:middle line:84%
And it's Eli Lilly who's doing
much less advertising than it

01:21:44.600 --> 01:21:47.395 align:middle line:84%
should, although Eli
Lilly does come in notably

01:21:47.395 --> 01:21:48.770 align:middle line:84%
and do a lot of
advertising right

01:21:48.770 --> 01:21:50.730 align:middle line:84%
in the very final
month of that sample.

01:21:50.730 --> 01:21:52.790 align:middle line:84%
So what he finds is
that firms should

01:21:52.790 --> 01:21:54.770 align:middle line:84%
be doing four times
as much advertising

01:21:54.770 --> 01:21:56.187 align:middle line:84%
as they could if
they were playing

01:21:56.187 --> 01:21:58.220 align:middle line:90%
the equilibrium of this game.

01:21:58.220 --> 01:22:00.050 align:middle line:84%
Second counterfactual
said, well,

01:22:00.050 --> 01:22:03.560 align:middle line:84%
what if they had an
advertising cooperative?

01:22:03.560 --> 01:22:05.570 align:middle line:84%
As he said, advertising
can be much less

01:22:05.570 --> 01:22:08.600 align:middle line:84%
than is optimal for the
firms, given that there are so

01:22:08.600 --> 01:22:11.960 align:middle line:84%
many spillovers and that
advertising by one firm

01:22:11.960 --> 01:22:14.660 align:middle line:84%
decreases the incentives of
the other firms to advertise.

01:22:14.660 --> 01:22:17.120 align:middle line:84%
And what he finds is that an
advertising cooperative would

01:22:17.120 --> 01:22:20.600 align:middle line:84%
then do yet two and a half
times more advertising than

01:22:20.600 --> 01:22:22.260 align:middle line:90%
would happen in equilibrium.

01:22:22.260 --> 01:22:25.490 align:middle line:84%
So that's 10 times the
current level of advertising

01:22:25.490 --> 01:22:27.885 align:middle line:84%
is what an advertising
cooperative would do.

01:22:27.885 --> 01:22:31.108 align:middle line:84%
What would be the
profit impact of that?

01:22:31.108 --> 01:22:33.150 align:middle line:84%
Raising advertising by a
factor of 10-- you know,

01:22:33.150 --> 01:22:35.560 align:middle line:84%
the advertising
does cost money--

01:22:35.560 --> 01:22:38.855 align:middle line:84%
ends up raising profits by
about 15% for the firms.

01:22:38.855 --> 01:22:41.860 align:middle line:90%


01:22:41.860 --> 01:22:45.790 align:middle line:84%
So I guess that's roughly
a summary of the paper

01:22:45.790 --> 01:22:47.810 align:middle line:90%
is that he has these two parts.

01:22:47.810 --> 01:22:52.720 align:middle line:84%
He's got the nice, simple causal
analysis part using this DMA

01:22:52.720 --> 01:22:54.660 align:middle line:90%
border variation.

01:22:54.660 --> 01:22:58.510 align:middle line:84%
DMA border variation tells us
that advertising is effective

01:22:58.510 --> 01:23:01.250 align:middle line:84%
and advertising crowds
out-rival advertising.

01:23:01.250 --> 01:23:03.320 align:middle line:84%
He then builds that
structural model.

01:23:03.320 --> 01:23:08.350 align:middle line:84%
The structural model
lets us put more detail

01:23:08.350 --> 01:23:09.943 align:middle line:90%
on what these curves look like.

01:23:09.943 --> 01:23:11.860 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, more detail
on what they look like,

01:23:11.860 --> 01:23:13.760 align:middle line:84%
given assumptions on
what they look like.

01:23:13.760 --> 01:23:16.240 align:middle line:84%
But the advantage of
putting that second part in

01:23:16.240 --> 01:23:18.910 align:middle line:84%
is that the second part, we
can't talk about optimality.

01:23:18.910 --> 01:23:20.500 align:middle line:84%
And the optimality,
what he finds

01:23:20.500 --> 01:23:22.420 align:middle line:84%
is that in equilibrium,
once the firms learn

01:23:22.420 --> 01:23:25.120 align:middle line:84%
how effective
advertising is, you

01:23:25.120 --> 01:23:27.740 align:middle line:84%
would expect them to advertise
much more than they do.

01:23:27.740 --> 01:23:29.200 align:middle line:84%
That seems like
that could explain

01:23:29.200 --> 01:23:30.700 align:middle line:84%
what's happened in
American TV where

01:23:30.700 --> 01:23:32.308 align:middle line:90%
we do see a lot of advertising.

01:23:32.308 --> 01:23:34.600 align:middle line:84%
And then he talks about the
magnitude of the free rider

01:23:34.600 --> 01:23:35.120 align:middle line:90%
effects.

01:23:35.120 --> 01:23:37.510 align:middle line:84%
And again, the free rider
effects are very large here,

01:23:37.510 --> 01:23:40.450 align:middle line:84%
and firms are greatly
advertising relative

01:23:40.450 --> 01:23:42.168 align:middle line:84%
to the firms'
collective interest.

01:23:42.168 --> 01:23:44.460 align:middle line:84%
Whether you think it's social
interest or not obviously

01:23:44.460 --> 01:23:46.280 align:middle line:84%
depends on whether
you think depression

01:23:46.280 --> 01:23:49.430 align:middle line:84%
is being undertreated or
overtreated with medications.

01:23:49.430 --> 01:23:52.970 align:middle line:84%
And what are the benefits
of that to people?

01:23:52.970 --> 01:23:53.780 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:23:53.780 --> 01:23:56.130 align:middle line:84%
So guess that's about
all I had for today.

01:23:56.130 --> 01:24:00.200 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to then just
finish up saying next class,

01:24:00.200 --> 01:24:03.270 align:middle line:84%
we're going to be back in the
theory empirical rotation.

01:24:03.270 --> 01:24:05.570 align:middle line:84%
I'll go back to talking
about theory work.

01:24:05.570 --> 01:24:08.330 align:middle line:84%
And I'll do three
theoretical papers

01:24:08.330 --> 01:24:09.650 align:middle line:90%
that are on search engines.

01:24:09.650 --> 01:24:11.330 align:middle line:84%
The Edelman,
Ostrovsky, Schwartz,

01:24:11.330 --> 01:24:15.510 align:middle line:84%
my paper with Susan Athey, and
Anderson and Renault's paper.

01:24:15.510 --> 01:24:18.770 align:middle line:84%
And then I will do one paper
by Armstrong and Zhou, which

01:24:18.770 --> 01:24:22.640 align:middle line:84%
is about retail platforms
and thinking about design

01:24:22.640 --> 01:24:26.170 align:middle line:84%
of a retail platform, how much
information to give consumers.

01:24:26.170 --> 01:24:43.000 align:middle line:90%